output:
We can solve the problem by simply simulating all possible swaps (there are only three possible swaps since there are three letters). For each test case, if the input string is already "abc", we output "YES". Otherwise, we try swapping every pair of positions and check if we obtain "abc". If one of these swaps yields "abc", we output "YES" and otherwise "NO".

Below is the complete C++ solution:

------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while(t--){
        string s;
        cin >> s;

        // If already matching "abc", no operations needed.
        if(s == "abc"){
            cout << "YES" << "\n";
            continue;
        }

        bool possible = false;
        // There are only 3 positions, try swapping any two cards.
        for(int i = 0; i < 3 && !possible; i++){
            for(int j = i+1; j < 3 && !possible; j++){
                string temp = s;
                swap(temp[i], temp[j]);
                if(temp == "abc"){
                    possible = true;
                }
            }
        }

        cout << (possible ? "YES" : "NO") << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. Read the number of test cases t.
2. For each test case:
   - Read the string s.
   - Check if s is already "abc". If yes, output "YES".
   - Otherwise, swap each pair of positions and check if the result becomes "abc".
   - If any swap works, print "YES". If none do, print "NO".

This solution works under the constraints given and correctly determines whether it is possible to obtain "abc" with at most one swap operation.

Below are the steps to add and set up a Toolbar inside a Fragment:

1. Create or update your fragment's XML layout to include a Toolbar (or MaterialToolbar). For example, in res/layout/fragment_example.xml:

--------------------------------------------------
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <!-- Toolbar at the top -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
        android:title="Fragment Title"
        app:titleTextColor="@android:color/white"/>

    <!-- Your fragment content below the toolbar -->
    <FrameLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:layout_marginTop="?attr/actionBarSize" />

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>
--------------------------------------------------

2. In your Fragment class, inflate the layout and set up the Toolbar. If you want the fragment’s toolbar to act as the ActionBar (for handling menu items or navigation), make sure your host Activity extends AppCompatActivity. Then, in your fragment’s code (Java or Kotlin), do the following:

Java Example:
--------------------------------------------------
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar;

public class ExampleFragment extends Fragment {

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        return inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);
    }

    @Override
    public void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState);

        Toolbar toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

        // Set the toolbar as the ActionBar for the hosting Activity
        AppCompatActivity activity = (AppCompatActivity) getActivity();
        if (activity != null) {
            activity.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);
            // Optionally, configure the home icon (if you need a back button, etc.)
            if (activity.getSupportActionBar() != null) {
                activity.getSupportActionBar().setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
                activity.getSupportActionBar().setDisplayShowHomeEnabled(true);
            }
        }

        // Optionally, if you want to add menu items:
        // Enable options menu in fragment
        setHasOptionsMenu(true);
    }

    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(@NonNull Menu menu, @NonNull MenuInflater inflater) {
        // Inflate the menu items for use in the action bar
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.example_menu, menu);
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------

Kotlin Example:
--------------------------------------------------
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar

class ExampleFragment : Fragment(R.layout.fragment_example) {

    override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)

        val toolbar = view.findViewById<Toolbar>(R.id.toolbar)
        (activity as? AppCompatActivity)?.apply {
            setSupportActionBar(toolbar)
            supportActionBar?.apply {
                setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true)
                setDisplayShowHomeEnabled(true)
            }
        }

        // Enable the fragment to add menu items to the host activity
        setHasOptionsMenu(true)
    }

    override fun onCreateOptionsMenu(menu: Menu, inflater: MenuInflater) {
        // Inflate the menu for the toolbar if needed
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.example_menu, menu)
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater)
    }
}
--------------------------------------------------

3. (Optional) If you need to handle toolbar menu item clicks directly within the fragment, override onOptionsItemSelected:
--------------------------------------------------
@Override
public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(@NonNull MenuItem item) {
    if (item.getItemId() == android.R.id.home) {
        // Handle navigation (for example, back button)
        getActivity().onBackPressed();
        return true;
    }
    // Handle other menu items here
    return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
}
--------------------------------------------------

4. (Optional) If you don’t wish to use the fragment’s toolbar as the activity’s ActionBar, you can simply handle view events on it, without calling setSupportActionBar. For example:
--------------------------------------------------
toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(v -> {
    // Handle navigation click event here
});
--------------------------------------------------

With these steps, you’ll have successfully added and set up a Toolbar inside your Fragment. Adjust styles, menu resources, and other properties as needed for your specific app design.

Below is one way to implement a non‐recursive deep search that looks for an object whose “name” property matches a target value. It uses an explicit stack to keep track of the objects to visit along with the “path” (i.e. the list of keys from the root) used to get there:

------------------------------------------------------------
"use strict";

// This function searches for an object (deeply nested in the given root object)
// that has a "name" property matching targetName.
// It uses an iterative (non‑recursive) approach and returns an object with two properties:
//   • found: the matching object (or null if not found)
//   • path: an array with the keys used to reach the found object.
function findObjectByName(root, targetName) {
  // Each entry in the stack has two properties:
  // - node: the current object to inspect
  // - path: an array of keys representing the path to that object from the root
  const stack = [];
  stack.push({ node: root, path: [] });

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    // Pop the next element from the stack
    const { node, path } = stack.pop();

    // Ensure we are dealing with an object (and not null)
    if (node !== null && typeof node === 'object') {
      // Check if the current object has the desired name property.
      if (node.name === targetName) {
        return { found: node, path: path };
      }

      // Iterate over own properties of the object
      for (const key in node) {
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(node, key)) {
          const child = node[key];
          // If the property is an object (and not null), add it to the stack.
          if (child !== null && typeof child === 'object') {
            // Create the new path by cloning the current path and appending the key.
            stack.push({ node: child, path: path.concat(key) });
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Return null if no object with the given name was found.
  return { found: null, path: [] };
}

// ---------------------------
// Example usage:
const data = {
  id: 1,
  name: "root",
  child: {
    id: 2,
    name: "child1",
    details: {
      id: 3,
      name: "target",  // The target object we want to locate.
      info: "some information"
    }
  },
  extra: {
    id: 4,
    name: "child2"
  }
};

const targetName = "target";
const result = findObjectByName(data, targetName);

if (result.found) {
  console.log("Found object:", result.found);
  console.log("Path to object:", result.path.join(" -> "));
} else {
  console.log("Object with name '" + targetName + "' not found.");
}

------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. We start by initializing a stack with an object containing the root node and an empty path.
2. In the while‑loop, we pop from the stack and check whether the current node’s "name" property matches the target.
3. If not, we push any child object (found by iterating over own properties) onto the stack, along with the path extended by the corresponding key.
4. Once a match is found, the function returns both the found object and the path used to reach it.
5. If no object is found after checking every object, we return a result with found set to null.

This solution avoids recursion and delivers the path used to reach the object, as requested.

