We can prove that S represents a fair distribution if and only if for every non‐empty suffix of S the difference between the number of ones (toys for Alice) and zeros (toys for Bob) is at most 1 (in absolute value).

Why is that? The idea is that since the toy values are nonnegative and in sorted (non‐decreasing) order, a worst‐case assignment (if one wanted to “break” the fairness guarantee) is to choose the toy prices from the set {0, x} (with x = vN, the maximum value) in the following “two‐phase” structure:
  • For some index t (possibly 0), assign v[1..t] = 0.
  • Then assign v[t+1..N] = x.
Because the values must be non–decreasing, such an assignment is legal.
Now, note that for indices i > t the value is x. So the contribution to Alice’s total (or Bob’s total) from these later toys is “x” times the count of toys that go to that kid in positions t+1..N.
Then the difference between Alice’s and Bob’s totals is
  x × ((# ones in positions t+1..N) – (# zeros in positions t+1..N)).
For fairness we require that for all t (which correspond to choosing any “jump point” in the toy values)
  | x × ((# ones in suffix) – (# zeros in suffix)) | ≤ x
Since x can be positive (and we can divide by x) this becomes
  | (# ones in suffix) – (# zeros in suffix) | ≤ 1.
Thus the condition “S is fair” is equivalent to that inequality holding for every non–empty suffix of S.

For example, consider the sample cases:
 • S = "1010" (N = 4). Its suffixes are:
  "1010": ones = 2, zeros = 2  → diff = 0;
  "010": ones = 1, zeros = 2  → diff = 1;
  "10": ones = 1, zeros = 1  → diff = 0;
  "0": ones = 0, zeros = 1  → diff = 1.
All differences are ≤ 1 so the distribution is fair.
 • S = "1100" (N = 4). Its suffix "00" (positions 3–4) has ones = 0 and zeros = 2 giving diff = 2 > 1. So it is not fair.

Below is a short C program that implements this check for T test cases. (Note: We allocate enough memory for the input string, then we scan from right to left accumulating counts, and check the condition.)

------------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <math.h>

int main(){
    int T;
    if(scanf("%d", &T) != 1) return 1;

    while(T--){
        int N;
        scanf("%d", &N);
        // Allocate memory for the string. +2 for newline safety and null terminator.
        char *s = (char*) malloc((N+2) * sizeof(char));
        scanf("%s", s);

        int ones = 0, zeros = 0;
        int fair = 1; // assume fair until proven otherwise

        // Process from the rightmost character to the leftmost.
        for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--){
            if(s[i] == '1')
                ones++;
            else
                zeros++;
            // Check fairness condition for the current suffix.
            if (abs(ones - zeros) > 1){
                fair = 0;
                break;
            }
        }

        printf("%s\n", fair ? "YES" : "NO");
        free(s);
    }
    return 0;
}

------------------------------------------------

Explanation of the code:
1. We first read the number of test cases T.
2. For each test case, we read the number of toys N and the binary string s.
3. We then initialize counters ones and zeros to count the numbers in the suffix.
4. Iterating from the end (rightmost) of the string to the beginning, we update the counts. If at any point the absolute difference |ones − zeros| exceeds 1, we set fair to false.
5. Finally, we print “YES” if fair remains true, or “NO” if not.

This simple program meets the problem requirements.

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int T, N, X;

    // Read the number of test cases
    scanf("%d", &T);

    while(T--) {
        // Read N and X for each test case.
        // There are 2N total players and X rated players.
        scanf("%d %d", &N, &X);

        // The best strategy is to pair as many rated players with unrated players.
        // There are 2N - X unrated players. So at most min(X, 2N - X)
        // rated players can be paired with unrated players.
        // If X > (2N - X), the remaining rated players that must be paired together are:
        // X - (2N - X) = 2X - 2N.
        // In such a pairing, each rated-vs-rated match counts 2 rated players.
        // Therefore, the minimal number of rated players having a rated opponent is:
        // if (X <= N) then 0 (since 2N-X >= X), else it's 2X - 2N.
        if(X <= N) {
            printf("0\n");
        } else {
            printf("%d\n", 2 * X - 2 * N);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}

Suppose you have:

• A parameter P1 defined over (A, B), i.e. P1(a,b)  
• A one‐to‐one mapping from each element in set B to exactly one element in set C. One common way to represent this mapping is to define a set (or parameter) that relates each b in B to its corresponding c in C. For example, you might have a binary indicator set called MAP such that
  MAP(b,c) = 1 if b maps to c, and zero otherwise.

Then you want to create a new parameter P2 defined over (A, C) such that for every a ∈ A and its mapped c ∈ C (which corresponds to some unique b ∈ B), the value of P2 is taken from P1. Since there’s a one‐to‐one mapping, you can “pick out” the right b by summing over b with an indicator.

A typical way to write this in GAMS is:

  P2(a,c) = sum( b$(MAP(b,c) = 1), P1(a,b));

Because MAP(b,c) is 1 only for the b that maps to c, this sum is really just copying the corresponding value from P1.

Here’s a small complete example:

------------------------------------------------
* Define sets
Sets
    a   / A1, A2, A3 /
    b   / B1, B2, B3 /
    c   / C1, C2, C3 /;

* Define the one-to-one mapping as a set (or parameter)
* For instance, let B1 map to C3, B2 map to C1, and B3 map to C2.
Set
    map(b,c) /
         B1.C3
         B2.C1
         B3.C2
    /;

* Parameter P1 defined over (a,b)
Parameter
    P1(a,b)    "Some parameter value";

* Here we assign some arbitrary numbers
P1('A1','B1') = 10;
P1('A1','B2') = 20;
P1('A1','B3') = 30;
P1('A2','B1') = 15;
P1('A2','B2') = 25;
P1('A2','B3') = 35;
P1('A3','B1') = 20;
P1('A3','B2') = 30;
P1('A3','B3') = 40;

* Now define parameter P2 over (a,c) and set its value using the mapping
Parameter
    P2(a,c)    "Mapped parameter";

* Create P2 by summing over b where map(b,c) is true.
P2(a,c) = sum( b$(map(b,c)), P1(a,b));

* Display the results
Display P2;
------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. The set MAP(b,c) is defined to hold those (b,c) pairs where b maps to c.
2. When we write P2(a,c) = sum( b$(map(b,c)), P1(a,b) ); for each fixed (a,c),
   the summation is over b but only those b for which map(b,c) is defined (or equals 1). Since the mapping is one-to-one, there is exactly one b for each c.
3. As a result, P2(a,c) gets the value of P1(a,b) for the unique b that maps to that specific c.

This is one common way in GAMS to “re-index” a parameter using a one-to-one mapping between indices.

