Hello, what do you think of this arduino code in regards to understandability, optimization and size?
Any suggestions for improvements?

void cycleLEDs(int interval) {
  const int nOutPins = sizeof(outPins) / sizeof(outPins[0]);
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int currIndex = 0; // Use static to retain value between function calls
  int nextIndex = 0;

  if (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval)) {

    // Determine direction
    int direction = interval < 0 ? -1 : 1;

    // Update secondary index
    nextIndex = currIndex + direction;
    if (nextIndex < 0) {
      nextIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (nextIndex >= nOutPins) {
      nextIndex = 0;
    }

    // Turn off all LEDs
    for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; i++) {
      if (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex){
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], HIGH);
      } else {
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], LOW);
      }      
    }

    // Update current index
    currIndex += direction;
    if (currIndex < 0) {
      currIndex = nOutPins - 1;
    } else if (currIndex >= nOutPins) {
      currIndex = 0;
    }

    // Update timer
    lastChange = millis();
  }
}

Thank you for your help, i value your input.

output:
Below is a review of your code regarding understandability, optimization, and size, along with some suggestions for improvements.

──────────────────────────────
1. Understandability

• Function Naming and Comments:  
 – The function name (cycleLEDs) is reasonably descriptive for its purpose.  
 – You’ve also added inline comments that explain what is happening (e.g., updating the index, toggling LEDs). This makes the code easier to follow.

• Static Variables:  
 – Using static variables (lastChange and currIndex) is appropriate here so that the function “remembers” its state between calls.  
 – The comments also explain their purpose.

──────────────────────────────
2. Optimization

• Using Modular Arithmetic:  
 – Both updating currIndex and nextIndex involve checking against bounds and wrapping around.  
 – You can simplify these if/else blocks by using modular arithmetic. For example:  
  currIndex = (currIndex + direction + nOutPins) % nOutPins;  
  This makes the code more concise and may also improve maintainability.

• Redundant Calculation of nextIndex:  
 – You calculate nextIndex and later update currIndex. Because nextIndex is computed as currIndex + direction (wrapped properly), you can directly set currIndex = nextIndex without recomputing similarly later. (Be sure that the order in which LEDs are updated still meets your design requirements.)  

• Handling millis() Rollover:  
 – When comparing time differences, it is generally recommended to subtract the previous timestamp from the current time (e.g., if (millis() - lastChange >= abs(interval)) …) to avoid issues when millis() rolls over. This is a standard practice in Arduino sketches.

──────────────────────────────
3. Size

• The code is already short and does what it needs to in only a few lines.  
 – The loop for updating each LED state is straightforward.  
 – Using static variables and local calculations don’t add significantly to size on Arduino platforms.

──────────────────────────────
4. Suggestions for Improvements

Below is one possible refactoring of your code incorporating the suggestions above:

--------------------------------------------------
void cycleLEDs(int interval) {
  const int nOutPins = sizeof(outPins) / sizeof(outPins[0]);
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int currIndex = 0;

  // Use subtraction to handle millis() rollover correctly.
  if (millis() - lastChange >= (unsigned int) abs(interval)) {
    // Determine direction
    int direction = (interval < 0) ? -1 : 1;
    // Calculate next index using modular arithmetic to wrap around.
    int nextIndex = (currIndex + direction + nOutPins) % nOutPins;

    // Optional: If your design needs both the current and next LED to be HIGH, update as follows:
    for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; i++) {
      if (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex) {
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], HIGH);
      } else {
        digitalWrite(outPins[i], LOW);
      }
    }

    // Update the current index to the next one.
    currIndex = nextIndex;

    // Update timer
    lastChange = millis();
  }
}
--------------------------------------------------

Additional notes:  
• Ensure that outPins is declared and populated properly in your code (presumably as a global array).  
• If you plan on only illuminating a single LED at a time rather than two simultaneously, adjust the loop accordingly.

──────────────────────────────
Conclusion

• The original code is straightforward and understandable.  
• Using modular arithmetic simplifies index wrapping.  
• Changing the millis() check to use subtraction improves robustness in case of a rollover.  
• Overall, the code is compact, and these small changes would refine its readability and efficiency without altering the design.

Feel free to ask if you need further clarifications or additional suggestions!

