output:
The issue is with the printf statement inside the ledAny function. The statement:

  printf("My pointer is %p\n", *ledA);

is dereferencing the pointer (using *ledA) which gives a boolean value instead of the pointer address. Since the "%p" format specifier expects a pointer, you should pass the pointer itself, like this:

  printf("My pointer is %p\n", ledA);

This way, you correctly print the memory address stored in the pointer ledA.

Below is an example PHP project that connects to a MySQL database named “Bob”, reads two fields (“field1” and “field2”) from an HTTP POST, and then stores them in a table. In this example, we assume that your database already has a table named “records” with at least the columns “field1” and “field2”. (If your table has a different name or structure, adjust the SQL accordingly.)

Below is the sample code (save it as, for example, index.php):

-------------------------------------------------------------
<?php
// Database connection settings
$host = "localhost";
$db   = "Bob";
$user = "your_username";   // Replace with your MySQL username
$pass = "your_password";   // Replace with your MySQL password

// Set DSN (Data Source Name)
$dsn = "mysql:host=$host;dbname=$db;charset=utf8mb4";

// PDO options (optional but recommended for error handling)
$options = [
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION,  // Throw exceptions on errors
    PDO::ATTR_DEFAULT_FETCH_MODE => PDO::FETCH_ASSOC,
    PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES => false,
];

try {
    // Create a PDO instance (connect to the database)
    $pdo = new PDO($dsn, $user, $pass, $options);
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    // If connection fails, display an error message and stop the script.
    die("Database connection failed: " . $e->getMessage());
}

// Check if the HTTP POST contains 'field1' and 'field2'
if (isset($_POST['field1']) && isset($_POST['field2'])) {
    // Retrieve the posted fields
    $field1 = $_POST['field1'];
    $field2 = $_POST['field2'];

    // Prepare an SQL statement to insert the data (using placeholders)
    $sql = "INSERT INTO records (field1, field2) VALUES (:field1, :field2)";
    $stmt = $pdo->prepare($sql);

    // Bind the actual values to the placeholders
    $stmt->bindParam(':field1', $field1);
    $stmt->bindParam(':field2', $field2);

    // Execute the prepared statement
    try {
        $stmt->execute();
        echo "Record inserted successfully!";
    } catch (PDOException $e) {
        echo "Error inserting record: " . $e->getMessage();
    }
} else {
    // If POST data is missing, display an informational message
    echo "Please provide both field1 and field2 via HTTP POST.";
}
?>
-------------------------------------------------------------

Instructions:

1. Replace "your_username" and "your_password" with your actual MySQL credentials.
2. Ensure the MySQL database named “Bob” exists on your MySQL server.
3. Create the table “records” (if it doesn’t exist) using a SQL command such as:

   CREATE TABLE records (
       id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
       field1 VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
       field2 VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
   );

4. To test, you can use an HTML form (for example, save as form.html) like this:

-------------------------------------------------------------
<html>
  <body>
    <form action="index.php" method="post">
      Field 1: <input type="text" name="field1"><br>
      Field 2: <input type="text" name="field2"><br>
      <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>
  </body>
</html>
-------------------------------------------------------------

Open form.html in your browser, fill in the fields, and when you click Submit the data will be sent via POST to index.php which will perform the database insertion.

This completes the PHP project.

Below is an example of a simple Chrome extension that adds a toolbar button. When you click the button, it retrieves the current page’s HTML content and saves it as an HTML file using the Chrome downloads API.

Follow these steps to create the extension:

─────────────────────────────  
1. Create a New Folder  
─────────────────────────────  
Create a new folder (e.g., “save-page-extension”) where you will place the following files.

─────────────────────────────  
2. Create manifest.json  
─────────────────────────────  
Create a file named manifest.json with the following content. (This example uses Manifest V3.)

--------------------------------------------------
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Contents",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the current page's content as an HTML file.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "scripting", "downloads"],
  "action": {
    "default_title": "Save Page"
  },
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  }
}
--------------------------------------------------

─────────────────────────────  
3. Create background.js  
─────────────────────────────  
Create a file named background.js with the following code. This code listens for the action (toolbar button click), injects a content extraction script into the current tab, and then saves the returned HTML content as “page.html” using the downloads API.

--------------------------------------------------
chrome.action.onClicked.addListener(async (tab) => {
  try {
    // Use the scripting API to execute the getPageContent function in the current tab
    const [{ result }] = await chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tab.id },
      function: getPageContent,
    });

    // Create a Blob from the retrieved HTML content
    const blob = new Blob([result], { type: "text/html" });
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

    // Use the downloads API to save the file
    chrome.downloads.download({
      url: url,
      filename: "page.html"
    });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to save page:", error);
  }
});

// This function will run in the context of the current page
function getPageContent() {
  return document.documentElement.outerHTML;
}
--------------------------------------------------

─────────────────────────────  
4. Load the Extension in Chrome  
─────────────────────────────  

• Open Chrome and navigate to chrome://extensions/  
• Enable “Developer mode” (toggle switch at the top-right)  
• Click “Load unpacked” and select your extension folder (“save-page-extension”)  

─────────────────────────────  
5. Test the Extension  
─────────────────────────────  

• Navigate to any web page you want to save.  
• Click the extension's toolbar button (it might show as a puzzle piece or the extension’s icon).  
• The extension will trigger and save the HTML content of the current page as “page.html” in your default downloads folder.

─────────────────────────────  
Notes  
─────────────────────────────  
• This extension simply saves the outer HTML of the page, which may not include certain dynamic or browser-rendered elements exactly as displayed.  
• For more advanced saving (e.g., embedding images or CSS), additional coding would be needed.  
• Make sure that if you deploy or distribute the extension that you follow all Google Chrome extension guidelines.

This completes the basic Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current page. Enjoy coding!

