Below is an example in C that demonstrates how to perform a blocking read that may be interrupted by a signal, and how to handle the EINTR error by retrying the read. In this example, we set up a signal handler for SIGUSR1 (you can use any signal that suits your application) and then call read() on STDIN_FILENO. If read() returns -1 and errno is EINTR, the operation is retried.

-------------------------
/* EINTR_example.c */
#include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void sigusr1_handler(int signum) {
    // This handler is just for demonstration purposes.
    // It prints a message and returns.
    write(STDOUT_FILENO, "SIGUSR1 caught!\n", 16);
}

int main(void) {
    struct sigaction sa;
    // Set up handler for SIGUSR1
    sa.sa_handler = sigusr1_handler;
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
    // Notice: Do not set SA_RESTART so that the blocking read can be interrupted.
    sa.sa_flags = 0;

    if (sigaction(SIGUSR1, &sa, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("sigaction");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    char buffer[1024];
    ssize_t nbytes;

    printf("Waiting for input (or send SIGUSR1 with 'kill -USR1 %d' from another terminal)...\n", getpid());

    // Try reading from STDIN. The read() may block until input is available.
    while (1) {
        nbytes = read(STDIN_FILENO, buffer, sizeof(buffer));
        if (nbytes < 0) {
            if (errno == EINTR) {
                // The read was interrupted by a signal.
                printf("read() was interrupted by a signal (EINTR), retrying...\n");
                continue; // Retry the read
            } else {
                perror("read");
                exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
            }
        }
        // nbytes == 0 means end-of-file
        break;
    }

    // If we reach here, read() was successful (or EOF was reached)
    if (nbytes > 0) {
        // Null-terminate the buffer before printing.
        if (nbytes < sizeof(buffer))
            buffer[nbytes] = '\0';
        else
            buffer[sizeof(buffer) - 1] = '\0';
        printf("Read %zd bytes: %s\n", nbytes, buffer);
    } else {
        printf("No bytes read (EOF reached).\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

-------------------------

Explanation:

1. We set the signal handler for SIGUSR1. Note that we intentionally do not set the SA_RESTART flag because we want the blocking read to be interrupted by the signal.
2. The program then calls read() on STDIN_FILENO. If a signal (SIGUSR1 in this case) is caught during the blocking read, read() returns -1 and errno is set to EINTR.
3. In the loop, when EINTR is detected, a message is printed and the read is retried.
4. If read() succeeds (or returns 0 for EOF), the loop breaks and the program prints the data that was read.

To test this code:
• Compile it with a C compiler (e.g., gcc -o EINTR_example EINTR_example.c).
• Run the executable in one terminal.
• From another terminal, send SIGUSR1 to the process (e.g., kill -USR1 <pid>).
• You will see the message from the signal handler and the read() will be retried.

This example shows a typical pattern for handling EINTR when a blocking system call such as read() is interrupted by a signal.

Below is an example of a simple C++ program that creates a UDP socket, binds it to port 888, and continuously reads incoming packets. Note that this code is written for POSIX‐compliant systems (Linux, macOS, etc.). If you are using Windows you’ll need to initialize Winsock and make small modifications.

----------------------
// udp_receiver.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>      // For memset()
#include <cstdlib>      // For exit()
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <unistd.h>     // For close()

#define PORT 888
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

int main() {
    // Create a UDP socket
    int sockfd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
    if (sockfd < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Cannot open socket." << std::endl;
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Setup the address structure for binding
    sockaddr_in addr;
    std::memset(&addr, 0, sizeof(addr));  // Zero out structure
    addr.sin_family = AF_INET;             // IPv4
    addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;     // Listen on all interfaces
    addr.sin_port = htons(PORT);           // Host-to-network short conversion

    // Bind the socket to port 888 on all interfaces
    if (bind(sockfd, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&addr), sizeof(addr)) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Bind failed." << std::endl;
        close(sockfd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    std::cout << "Listening for UDP packets on port " << PORT << "..." << std::endl;

    // Buffer to hold incoming data
    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];

    // Continuously receive packets
    while (true) {
        sockaddr_in senderAddr;
        socklen_t senderAddrLen = sizeof(senderAddr);
        ssize_t recvLen = recvfrom(sockfd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE - 1, 0,
                                   reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&senderAddr), &senderAddrLen);
        if (recvLen < 0) {
            std::cerr << "Error: recvfrom() failed." << std::endl;
            break;
        }

        // Null-terminate the received data and print it
        buffer[recvLen] = '\0';
        std::cout << "Received packet from " << inet_ntoa(senderAddr.sin_addr)
                  << ":" << ntohs(senderAddr.sin_port) << " -> " << buffer << std::endl;
    }

    // Clean up and close the socket
    close(sockfd);
    return 0;
}
----------------------

Compilation and running instructions (on Linux/macOS):

1. Compile:
   g++ udp_receiver.cpp -o udp_receiver

2. Run (you might need sudo if binding to privileged ports, but port 888 is unprivileged):
   ./udp_receiver

This program will continuously receive UDP packets on port 888 and print their content along with the sender’s address. If you intended to work with a TCP socket instead, you would need to modify the code to listen for connections and then use accept() to retrieve connected sockets.

Below is one acceptable solution. In this expression we define a 500×500 square with five vertices, but we “round” the top‐right corner by splitting that corner into two vertices and giving them nonzero tangents. (In our example the roundness uses a radius r = 50; you can change that value if needed.) 

Here’s the expression you can put on the Path property of a shape layer:

------------------------------------------------------------
r = 50;                              // radius of the rounded corner
k = 0.5522847498;                    // k value for a good quarter‐circle approximation

// Define our vertices in clockwise order.
// The square would normally have vertices at [0,0], [500,0], [500,500], [0,500].
// We “round” the top right by replacing [500,0] with two points:
//   one at [500 - r, 0] and the other at [500, r].
v = [
  [0, 0],         // top-left (sharp)
  [500 - r, 0],   // beginning of rounded top-right corner
  [500, r],       // end of rounded top-right corner
  [500, 500],     // bottom-right (sharp)
  [0, 500]        // bottom-left (sharp)
];

// Define In Tangents for each vertex.
// For a sharp corner the tangent is [0,0].
// For our rounded corner, we only need a tangent at the vertex [500, r] (the
// incoming tangent for the Bezier that approximates the quarter circle).
inT = [
  [0, 0],
  [0, 0],
  [0, -r*k],   // control point coming from above: a quarter-circle needs this value 
  [0, 0],
  [0, 0]
];

// Define Out Tangents for each vertex.
// The only nonzero tangent is for the vertex [500 - r, 0] (the outgoing tangent).
outT = [
  [0, 0],
  [r*k, 0],   // control point going to the right
  [0, 0],
  [0, 0],
  [0, 0]
];

createPath(v, inT, outT, true);
------------------------------------------------------------

How It Works:
1. We set a radius r for our rounded corner. The constant k (≈0.5523) is a standard value used to approximate a quarter‐circle with a Bezier curve.
2. Instead of one vertex at (500,0), we create two vertices: one at (500‑r,0) and one at (500,r). These two vertices, with their out/in tangents respectively, form the rounded (curved) top right corner.
3. The call to createPath creates a closed path from our vertex, in-tangent, and out-tangent arrays.

You can paste this expression into the expression box for the Path property of a shape layer’s shape group (after creating a shape layer with a “Path” property).

Any expression that produces an identical shape (500×500 square with a rounded top-right corner) is acceptable.

Below is one example of how you might approach this problem. Note that Open XML “pages” aren’t real parts of the document markup (they’re computed by the layout engine), so to extract “first two pages” you must have some means (for example, explicit page breaks or bookmarks) to mark the boundaries. In the sample below we assume that the first document uses explicit page‐break paragraphs to separate pages and that the “glossary” in the second document is bookmarked or otherwise identifiable. (You’ll need to adjust the extraction code to fit your documents’ structure.) Also note that copying headers/footers, images and styles between documents can be complex because they live in separate parts; the sample below uses helper routines to “clone” parts from one document into another.

Below is a sample C# function using Open XML SDK 2.5 (and helper methods) that demonstrates one way to merge the two documents as specified:

-----------------------------------------------------------
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using DocumentFormat.OpenXml;
using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging;
using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Wordprocessing;

namespace WordMergeSample
{
    public class WordMerger
    {
        /// <summary>
        /// Merges two Word documents into one.
        /// From sourceFile1, the function takes:
        ///   • the content that constitutes the “first two pages” 
        ///   • the headers and footers
        ///   • the document styles (fonts, design, etc.)
        /// From sourceFile2, it takes:
        ///   • the page content (body paragraphs)
        ///   • the “glossary” (assumed to be bookmarked with "Glossary")
        /// Both files may contain images.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="sourceFile1">Path to the first source file.</param>
        /// <param name="sourceFile2">Path to the second source file.</param>
        /// <param name="destinationFile">Path to the output merged file.</param>
        public static void MergeDocuments(string sourceFile1, string sourceFile2, string destinationFile)
        {
            // Create a new document for the destination.
            using (WordprocessingDocument destDoc =
                WordprocessingDocument.Create(destinationFile, DocumentFormat.OpenXml.WordprocessingDocumentType.Document))
            {
                // Add a MainDocumentPart and create the document structure.
                MainDocumentPart mainPart = destDoc.AddMainDocumentPart();
                // Add a new document with an empty body.
                mainPart.Document = new Document(new Body());

                // Copy styles from the first document to preserve fonts and design.
                using (WordprocessingDocument srcDoc1 = WordprocessingDocument.Open(sourceFile1, false))
                {
                    // Copy styles part if available.
                    if (srcDoc1.MainDocumentPart.StyleDefinitionsPart != null)
                    {
                        StyleDefinitionsPart destStyles = mainPart.AddNewPart<StyleDefinitionsPart>();
                        destStyles.FeedData(srcDoc1.MainDocumentPart.StyleDefinitionsPart.GetStream());
                    }

                    // Copy numbering definitions (if any).
                    if (srcDoc1.MainDocumentPart.NumberingDefinitionsPart != null)
                    {
                        NumberingDefinitionsPart destNumbering =
                            mainPart.AddNewPart<NumberingDefinitionsPart>();
                        destNumbering.FeedData(srcDoc1.MainDocumentPart.NumberingDefinitionsPart.GetStream());
                    }

                    // Copy header and footer parts from source1.
                    CopyHeadersAndFooters(srcDoc1, mainPart);

                    // Extract the first 2 pages (using our own heuristic method).
                    List<OpenXmlElement> firstTwoPagesContent =
                        ExtractContentFromFirstTwoPages(srcDoc1.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body);

                    // Append the extracted content into the destination body.
                    foreach (var element in firstTwoPagesContent)
                    {
                        mainPart.Document.Body.Append(element.CloneNode(true));
                    }
                }

                // Insert a page break between the two source documents.
                mainPart.Document.Body.AppendChild(new Paragraph(new Run(new Break() { Type = BreakValues.Page })));

                // Process the second document.
                using (WordprocessingDocument srcDoc2 = WordprocessingDocument.Open(sourceFile2, false))
                {
                    // For the contents, we assume the “body” paragraphs are desired.
                    // You might filter these further if some paragraphs are not desired.
                    Body srcBody = srcDoc2.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;
                    List<OpenXmlElement> bodyContent = new List<OpenXmlElement>();
                    foreach (var element in srcBody.Elements())
                    {
                        // If the glossary is marked by a bookmark named “Glossary”, we extract it later.
                        // Otherwise, assume that the entire content is valid.
                        bodyContent.Add(element.CloneNode(true));
                    }
                    foreach (var element in bodyContent)
                    {
                        mainPart.Document.Body.Append(element);
                    }

                    // Extract the glossary content.
                    // In this example, we assume the glossary is delimited by a bookmark with name "Glossary".
                    List<OpenXmlElement> glossaryContent = ExtractGlossary(srcDoc2);
                    if (glossaryContent.Count > 0)
                    {
                        // Optionally insert a heading before the glossary.
                        Paragraph heading = new Paragraph(new Run(new Text("Glossary")));
                        mainPart.Document.Body.Append(heading);

                        foreach (var element in glossaryContent)
                        {
                            mainPart.Document.Body.Append(element.CloneNode(true));
                        }
                    }

                    // Copy any needed image parts from source2.
                    // This sample does not demonstrate the detailed remapping needed for images,
                    // but if you use CloneNode(true), images referenced in the source content
                    // must be copied to the destination and their relationship IDs updated.
                    // You might use helper libraries like OpenXmlPowerTools to ease this.
                }

                mainPart.Document.Save();
            }
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Copies all header and footer parts from the source document to the destination main part,
        /// and updates the section properties of the main document accordingly.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CopyHeadersAndFooters(WordprocessingDocument srcDoc, MainDocumentPart destMainPart)
        {
            // Get the section properties from source.
            SectionProperties srcSectProps = srcDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body.Elements<SectionProperties>().LastOrDefault();
            if (srcSectProps == null)
                return;

            SectionProperties destSectProps = (SectionProperties)srcSectProps.CloneNode(true);

            // Process headers.
            foreach (HeaderReference headerRef in srcSectProps.Descendants<HeaderReference>())
            {
                // Get the header part using the Id attribute.
                HeaderPart srcHeaderPart = (HeaderPart)srcDoc.MainDocumentPart.GetPartById(headerRef.Id);
                // Add a new header part to the destination.
                HeaderPart destHeaderPart = destMainPart.AddNewPart<HeaderPart>();
                destHeaderPart.FeedData(srcHeaderPart.GetStream());
                // Update the reference Id.
                headerRef.Id = destMainPart.GetIdOfPart(destHeaderPart);
            }

            // Process footers.
            foreach (FooterReference footerRef in srcSectProps.Descendants<FooterReference>())
            {
                // Get the footer part from source.
                FooterPart srcFooterPart = (FooterPart)srcDoc.MainDocumentPart.GetPartById(footerRef.Id);
                // Add a new footer part to destination.
                FooterPart destFooterPart = destMainPart.AddNewPart<FooterPart>();
                destFooterPart.FeedData(srcFooterPart.GetStream());
                // Update the reference Id.
                footerRef.Id = destMainPart.GetIdOfPart(destFooterPart);
            }

            // Append the (updated) section properties to the destination document.
            destMainPart.Document.Body.Append(destSectProps);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Extract content from the first two “pages” of the document body.
        /// In this example, we assume that each page is separated by a Break element of type Page.
        /// Adjust this method if your documents define page breaks differently.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="body">The document body.</param>
        /// <returns>A list of OpenXmlElements representing the first two pages.</returns>
        private static List<OpenXmlElement> ExtractContentFromFirstTwoPages(Body body)
        {
            List<OpenXmlElement> result = new List<OpenXmlElement>();
            int pageCount = 1; // start with page 1

            foreach (var element in body.Elements())
            {
                // Add the element to current page.
                result.Add(element.CloneNode(true));

                // If the element contains any run with a page break, then increase count.
                if (element.Descendants<Break>().Any(b => b.Type != null && b.Type == BreakValues.Page))
                {
                    pageCount++;
                    if (pageCount > 2) break;  // Stop once we've grabbed two pages.
                }
            }
            return result;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Extract the glossary content from the source document.
        /// This sample assumes that the glossary is bookmarked with the name "Glossary"
        /// (i.e. there is a BookmarkStart/BookmarkEnd pair with Name="Glossary").
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="srcDoc">The source WordprocessingDocument.</param>
        /// <returns>A list of OpenXmlElements representing the glossary content.</returns>
        private static List<OpenXmlElement> ExtractGlossary(WordprocessingDocument srcDoc)
        {
            List<OpenXmlElement> glossaryElements = new List<OpenXmlElement>();
            Body body = srcDoc.MainDocumentPart.Document.Body;

            // Find the bookmark start with name "Glossary".
            var bookmarkStart = body.Descendants<BookmarkStart>()
                                    .FirstOrDefault(b => b.Name == "Glossary");
            if (bookmarkStart == null)
                return glossaryElements;

            // We assume the bookmark covers consecutive siblings.
            bool withinBookmark = false;
            foreach (var element in body.Elements())
            {
                if (element.Descendants<BookmarkStart>().Any(b => b.GetAttribute("id", "").Value == bookmarkStart.Id))
                {
                    withinBookmark = true;
                }
                if (withinBookmark)
                {
                    glossaryElements.Add(element.CloneNode(true));
                    if (element.Descendants<BookmarkEnd>().Any(b => b.Id == bookmarkStart.Id))
                        break;
                }
            }
            return glossaryElements;
        }
    }
}

-----------------------------------------------------------

Notes and caveats:
1. Pagination: Since WordprocessingML does not have “page” elements, the ExtractContentFromFirstTwoPages method here simply looks for Break elements of type Page. If your document does not use explicit page breaks or uses more complex pagination (like sections), you may need to use a more advanced method (or even a conversion to PDF and back, or a third‐party layout engine).

2. Images and Relationships: Cloning content that includes images (or other external parts) requires copying their associated parts (such as image parts) as well as updating relationship IDs in the document XML so that the images in the destination point to the newly added parts. Production code would need to inspect each element (for example, a Drawing element) and re-map its relationships. Solutions like OpenXmlPowerTools (DocumentBuilder) may simplify this process.

3. Styles and Numbering: The code above copies the styles and numbering parts from the first document so that fonts and design are preserved. If the second document’s content uses different styles, you might need additional merging logic.

4. Headers/Footers: The sample copies headers and footers only from the first document. Make sure the destination document’s section properties refer to these copied parts.

This sample should serve as a starting point. Depending on the actual file structure and requirements, you may need to extend the helper functions to properly re-map image references and other external parts.

