Below is an example of a Laravel middleware that checks if the request IP exists in a predefined array of allowed IP addresses. If the IP is not allowed, the middleware aborts the request with a 403 (Forbidden) status.

1. Create a new middleware using Artisan (optional):
   Run the command:
     php artisan make:middleware CheckIp

2. Open the newly created file at app/Http/Middleware/CheckIp.php and update it as follows:

---------------------------------------------------
<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class CheckIp
{
    /**
     * Array of allowed IP addresses.
     *
     * @var array
     */
    protected $allowedIps = [
        '127.0.0.1',    // Localhost (IPv4)
        '::1',          // Localhost (IPv6)
        // Add more allowed IP addresses as needed
        // '192.168.1.100',
    ];

    /**
     * Handle an incoming request.
     *
     * @param  \Illuminate\Http\Request  $request
     * @param  \Closure  $next
     * @return mixed
     */
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next)
    {
        // Check if the request IP exists in the allowed IPs array.
        if (! in_array($request->ip(), $this->allowedIps)) {
            // Optionally, you can log this unauthorized access attempt
            // Log::warning("Unauthorized IP attempted access: " . $request->ip());

            // Abort the request with a 403 Forbidden response.
            abort(403, 'Unauthorized IP address.');
        }

        // Continue handling the request if the IP is allowed.
        return $next($request);
    }
}
---------------------------------------------------

3. Register the middleware:

   - If you want this middleware to apply globally across your application, add it to the $middleware array in app/Http/Kernel.php.
   - If you want to assign it to specific routes or route groups, add it to the $routeMiddleware array in app/Http/Kernel.php. For example:

   In app/Http/Kernel.php:
   ---------------------------------------------------
   protected $routeMiddleware = [
       // Other middleware...
       'check.ip' => \App\Http\Middleware\CheckIp::class,
   ];
   ---------------------------------------------------

4. Use the middleware on routes:

   If you registered the middleware using the key 'check.ip', you can apply it to your routes like so:
   ---------------------------------------------------
   Route::get('/your-route', function () {
       // Your route logic...
   })->middleware('check.ip');
   ---------------------------------------------------

Now, any request to a route protected by the CheckIp middleware will first verify that the request's IP is included in the allowed IP list. If it isn't, the request will be terminated with a 403 error.

