Below is one way to implement the backend. In this example, we create a FavoritesManager class with methods to add, remove, and list favorites. When the user is logged in the favorites are saved in the database (using the Favorite model), and when the user is a guest they are saved to the session. Then we create a FavoritesController that serves as an API, exposing endpoints for listing, adding, and removing favorites. (Later you can use Laravel routes to map API endpoints to these controller methods.)

NOTE: This example assumes that you have a favorites table (and matching Favorite model) with at least a user_id (nullable) and a name (or business_name) column. Also, feel free to adapt validation and error handling to your needs.

────────────────────────────
File: app/Favorites/FavoritesManager.php

<?php

namespace App\Favorites;

use App\Models\Favorite;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Auth;

class FavoritesManager
{
    protected $request;

    public function __construct(Request $request)
    {
        $this->request = $request;
    }

    /**
     * Add a business name as favorite.
     *
     * @param string $businessName
     * @return mixed
     */
    public function add(string $businessName)
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            // For logged in users, add to the database.
            // Using firstOrCreate to avoid duplicate initial inserts.
            return Favorite::firstOrCreate([
                'user_id' => Auth::id(),
                'name'    => $businessName,
            ]);
        } else {
            // For guests, add to the session.
            $favorites = $this->request->session()->get('favorites', []);

            // Only add if not already present.
            if (!in_array($businessName, $favorites)) {
                $favorites[] = $businessName;
                $this->request->session()->put('favorites', $favorites);
            }
            return $favorites;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Remove a business name from favorites.
     *
     * @param string $businessName
     * @return bool
     */
    public function remove(string $businessName): bool
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            Favorite::where('user_id', Auth::id())
                ->where('name', $businessName)
                ->delete();
            return true;
        } else {
            $favorites = $this->request->session()->get('favorites', []);
            if (($key = array_search($businessName, $favorites)) !== false) {
                unset($favorites[$key]);
                // reindex the array
                $favorites = array_values($favorites);
                $this->request->session()->put('favorites', $favorites);
            }
            return true;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Get the list of favorite business names.
     *
     * @return array|\Illuminate\Support\Collection
     */
    public function all()
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            // Return only the names (adjust if you need more fields)
            return Favorite::where('user_id', Auth::id())->pluck('name');
        } else {
            return $this->request->session()->get('favorites', []);
        }
    }
}

────────────────────────────
File: app/Http/Controllers/FavoritesController.php

<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Favorites\FavoritesManager;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class FavoritesController extends Controller
{
    protected $favoritesManager;

    public function __construct(FavoritesManager $favoritesManager)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager = $favoritesManager;
    }

    /**
     * Return the list of favorite names.
     *
     * GET /api/favorites
     *
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse
     */
    public function index()
    {
        $favorites = $this->favoritesManager->all();
        return response()->json(['favorites' => $favorites]);
    }

    /**
     * Add a favorite business name.
     *
     * POST /api/favorites/add
     * Expected payload: { "name": "Example Business" }
     *
     * @param Request $request
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse
     */
    public function add(Request $request)
    {
        $request->validate([
            'name' => 'required|string',
        ]);

        $businessName = $request->input('name');
        $this->favoritesManager->add($businessName);

        return response()->json([
            'success'   => true,
            'message'   => "Favorite added successfully.",
            'favorites' => $this->favoritesManager->all()
        ]);
    }

    /**
     * Remove a favorite business name.
     *
     * DELETE /api/favorites/remove
     * Expected payload: { "name": "Example Business" }
     *
     * @param Request $request
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse
     */
    public function remove(Request $request)
    {
        $request->validate([
            'name' => 'required|string',
        ]);

        $businessName = $request->input('name');
        $this->favoritesManager->remove($businessName);

        return response()->json([
            'success'   => true,
            'message'   => "Favorite removed successfully.",
            'favorites' => $this->favoritesManager->all()
        ]);
    }
}

────────────────────────────
Routing Example

In your routes file (typically routes/api.php), you could add routes like:

Route::get('/favorites', [FavoritesController::class, 'index']);
Route::post('/favorites/add', [FavoritesController::class, 'add']);
Route::delete('/favorites/remove', [FavoritesController::class, 'remove']);

────────────────────────────
Notes

1. Dependency Injection: The FavoritesManager is automatically injected into the controller thanks to Laravel’s service container. The FavoritesManager itself is also provided with the Request instance, so the session is available.

2. Database Favorites: For authenticated users, we assume that the Favorite model has a user_id and name field. You might want to adjust the model relations (e.g., add a favorites() relationship in your User model) as needed for your project.

3. Session Favorites: The guest’s favorites are simply stored as an array in the session under the “favorites” key. When a guest later logs in, you might want to merge the session favorites into the user’s database favorites if required.

4. API Responses: The controller methods return JSON responses so that the Vue frontend can work with the API.

This implementation should give you a solid starting point for handling favorites in both guest and authenticated contexts while making it easy to hook up with your Vue/Inertia frontend later on.

