This JavaScript code is designed to control a multi-step form on a webpage. It does so by showing one step at a time and allowing users to navigate forward ("next") or backward ("prev") between steps. Let’s break down the code:

1. Variables and DOM selection:

 • The line

  const steps = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("form .step"));

  finds all elements that have the class "step" inside a <form> and turns the NodeList into a standard array. Each element in this array represents a step or section in your multi-step form.

 • Next, the code selects all the “next” and “previous” buttons:

  const nextBtn = document.querySelectorAll("form .next-btn");
  const prevBtn = document.querySelectorAll("form .previous-btn");

  These are likely buttons attached to each step to navigate to the next or previous section.

 • Finally, the code selects the form element itself:

  const form = document.querySelector("form");

2. Adding Event Listeners to "Next" buttons:

 • The code iterates over each element in nextBtn and attaches a click event listener:

  nextBtn.forEach((button) => {
   button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    changeStep("next");
   });
  });

 • When any "next" button is clicked, the changeStep function is called with the argument "next". This tells the function that it should move forward one step.

3. Adding Event Listeners to "Previous" buttons:

 • Similarly, it attaches click event listeners to each "prev" button:

  prevBtn.forEach((button) => {
   button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    changeStep("prev");
   });
  });

 • When a "prev" button is clicked, the changeStep function is called with the argument "prev", indicating that the user should be taken back one step.

4. Handling Form Submission:

 • The form’s submit event is intercepted by adding this event listener:

  form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
   e.preventDefault();

   const inputs = [];
   // Here, the code gathers all input field values in the form.
   form.querySelectorAll("input").forEach((input) => {
    const { name, value } = input;
    inputs.push({ name, value });
   });
   console.log(inputs);
   form.reset();
  });

 • e.preventDefault() stops the form from doing its default action (for example, sending data to the server and refreshing the page).
 • The code then finds all input fields in the form. For each input, it extracts its name and value and pushes an object with these properties into the inputs array.
 • After gathering all the values, the inputs array is logged to the console.
 • Finally, form.reset() resets the form, clearing all the fields.

5. The changeStep Function:

 function changeStep(btn) {
  let index = 0;
  const active = document.querySelector(".active");
  index = steps.indexOf(active);

  steps[index].classList.remove("active");

  if (btn === "next") {
   index++;
  } else if (btn === "prev") {
   index--;
  }

  steps[index].classList.add("active");
 }

 • The function changeStep receives a parameter btn which is either the string "next" or "prev".
 • It starts by finding the currently active step (the one with the .active class) via document.querySelector(".active").
 • It then determines the index (position) of this active step in the steps array.
 • The code removes the "active" class from the current step.
 • Based on the btn parameter:
  - If "next", the index is increased to move forward.
  - If "prev", the index is decreased to move backward.
 • Finally, the new step at the updated index is given the class "active", making it visible as the current step.

Summary:
• The code sets up a multi-step form by keeping track of different sections (.step elements).
• It provides event listeners for going to the next and previous steps.
• It collects data from input fields when the form is submitted, logs it, and then resets the form.
• The changeStep function handles switching between the form steps by toggling the "active" class.

This overall mechanism creates a smooth navigation through multiple sections within a single form and gathers user input without refreshing the page.

