The problem is that by “hijacking” the form’s submit via an action URL (or inline JavaScript) you bypass the browser’s built‐in validation. In other words, when you use

  action="javascript:fn.popupActionHandler.submit('editFormPhoto')"

the browser does not run its default validation (which checks attributes such as minlength and maxlength).

A good solution is to remove the javascript: URL from the action and attach a submit handler that first verifies the validation before calling your custom submit code. For example, you can do one of the following:

─────────────────────────────  
Option 1. Use inline onsubmit with a helper function

Update your form to remove the action and add an onsubmit attribute. For example:

  <form id="editFormPhoto" method="post" onsubmit="return submitEditForm(event)">
     <input name="p" type="hidden" value="photo" />
     <input name="a" type="hidden" value="editPhoto" />
     <input name="id" type="hidden"  />
     <!-- Other fields here -->
     <!-- Submit button etc. -->
  </form>

Then, in your script add:

  <script>
    function submitEditForm(event) {
      event.preventDefault();            // stop the browser from doing a normal submit
      var form = event.target;
      // if the native HTML5 constraints are not met, show errors and do not continue
      if (!form.checkValidity()) {
        form.reportValidity();           // shows the built-in error messages
        return false;
      }
      // If valid, then run your custom submit handler:
      fn.popupActionHandler.submit('editFormPhoto');
      return false;
    }
  </script>

─────────────────────────────  
Option 2. Attach a submit event listener in JavaScript

Another approach is to remove the inline javascript from the action attribute and add a submit listener in your script. For example, change your form tag to:

  <form id="editFormPhoto" method="post">
     <!-- form fields -->
  </form>

And then in your script do:

  <script>
    document.getElementById('editFormPhoto').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
      event.preventDefault();    // prevent default form submission
      if (!this.checkValidity()) {
        this.reportValidity();   // show the error messages automatically
        return;
      }
      // If the form is valid, then call your custom submit function:
      fn.popupActionHandler.submit('editFormPhoto');
    });
  </script>

─────────────────────────────  
Explanation

• By using the form’s checkValidity() method we let the browser run its built‐in validations for attributes like minlength, maxlength, required, etc.

• When checkValidity() returns false, reportValidity() displays the error messages. Only if everything is valid do we proceed to execute fn.popupActionHandler.submit('editFormPhoto').

• Removing the “action=”javascript:…” attribute (and not using the novalidate attribute) ensures that the browser validation takes place.

Either approach fixes the issue and allows the minlength and maxlength attributes (as well as any other built‐in validations) to work on submit.

