Below is one example of a Vitis HLS–style C/C++ implementation of an MNIST classifier using a small neural network. In this example a two‐layer (fully connected) network is implemented with one hidden layer (with ReLU activation) and an output layer (producing “logits” for 10 classes). (In a production design you would load the actual trained weight and bias values into on‐chip memory or from an external source.) Note that this “toy” example uses constant arrays for weights and biases that are only initialized with dummy values. You would normally generate these weights from an offline training process (for example, using Python/TensorFlow or PyTorch) and then include them here.

Below is the complete code with HLS directives and detailed comments:

---------------------------------------------------------------
/* mnist_nn.cpp

   This Vitis HLS code implements a simple two‐layer neural network for handwriting digit classification.
   The network takes an input vector with 784 elements (28×28 MNIST images flattened) and computes:

     - A hidden layer of 128 neurons with a ReLU activation.
     - An output layer of 10 neurons producing logits (one for each digit).

   The top-level function “mnist_nn” is synthesized using Vitis HLS and the interfaces (AXI, control, etc.)
   can be mapped to a suitable FPGA IP.

   IMPORTANT:
   • The weight arrays and biases in this example are initialized to constant dummy values.
   • To use this for real inference, replace these constants with the trained parameters.
*/

#include <hls_stream.h>
#include "ap_int.h"

// Network dimensions
#define N_IN     784
#define N_HIDDEN 128
#define N_OUT    10

// Simple ReLU activation
inline float relu(float x) {
    return (x >= 0) ? x : 0;
}

// Top-level function to be synthesized
extern "C" void mnist_nn(float input[N_IN], float output[N_OUT]) {
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi     port=input  offset=slave bundle=gmem
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi     port=output offset=slave bundle=gmem
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=input                   bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=output                  bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return                  bundle=control

    // Local buffer for the hidden layer activations
    float hidden[N_HIDDEN];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=hidden complete

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Weight and bias arrays for fully connected layers.
    // Note: For demonstration, we initialize them with dummy values.
    // For example, we set every weight to 0.01 and every bias to 0.1.
    // In a real application these arrays would contain the trained parameters.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------

    static const float fc1_weights[N_HIDDEN][N_IN] = { 
        #include "fc1_weights.txt" // Optionally include a file containing trained weights.
        // Alternatively, initialize with dummy values:
        // [0 ... N_HIDDEN-1][0 ... N_IN-1] = 0.01f (not literally written out)
    };

    static const float fc1_bias[N_HIDDEN] = {
        #include "fc1_bias.txt"
        // Or, for dummy initialization:
        // 0.1f, 0.1f, ..., 0.1f
    };

    static const float fc2_weights[N_OUT][N_HIDDEN] = { 
        #include "fc2_weights.txt"
        // Or dummy: 0.01f for every element.
    };

    static const float fc2_bias[N_OUT] = {
        #include "fc2_bias.txt"
        // Or dummy: 0.1f for every element.
    };

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // First fully connected layer: input -> hidden with ReLU activation.
    // hidden[j] = ReLU( fc1_bias[j] + Sum(i=0 to 783){ input[i] * fc1_weights[j][i] } )
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    fc1_loop: for (int j = 0; j < N_HIDDEN; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        float sum = fc1_bias[j];
        fc1_inner: for (int i = 0; i < N_IN; i++) {
            sum += input[i] * fc1_weights[j][i];
        }
        hidden[j] = relu(sum);
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Second fully connected layer: hidden -> output.
    // output[k] = fc2_bias[k] + Sum(j=0 to 127){ hidden[j] * fc2_weights[k][j] }
    // In a full network you might then apply a softmax, but here we return the logits.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    fc2_loop: for (int k = 0; k < N_OUT; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        float sum = fc2_bias[k];
        fc2_inner: for (int j = 0; j < N_HIDDEN; j++) {
            sum += hidden[j] * fc2_weights[k][j];
        }
        output[k] = sum;
    }
}
---------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation of Key Elements:

1. Interface Pragmas:
   • The m_axi pragmas indicate that the input and output arrays are mapped to AXI memory interfaces.
   • The s_axilite pragmas are used for control (register-based) interfaces.

2. Weight/Bias Initialization:
   • In this example the weight arrays (fc1_weights and fc2_weights) and biases (fc1_bias, fc2_bias) are declared as static constant arrays.
   • In a full design you would supply the actual numbers from training. One common method is to generate text files containing the numbers and include them.

3. Loop Directives:
   • The HLS PIPELINE directive inside the loops (with an initiation interval of 1) enables pipelining of the loops to get higher throughput.

4. Activation Function:
   • The simple ReLU is implemented inline. More complicated activations might use lookup tables or other approximations.

How to Use:
• Customize the weight array initializations with the trained values.
• Use Vitis HLS to synthesize the design and generate an IP core.
• Integrate the IP core into your overall system (e.g. on a Zynq or Versal device) and transfer the MNIST input data via the AXI port.

This code provides a starting point for implementing an MNIST handwritten-digit classifier using Vitis HLS. Adjust the network dimensions, weight initializations, and HLS directives based on your performance and resource requirements.

