Below is one way to implement an infinite prime number stream in Rust. In this example, we define a custom iterator (struct PrimeIter) that uses trial division by storing all primes found so far. Each call to next() finds the next prime number and yields it.

------------------------------------------------------------
use std::f64;

/// A prime iterator that yields prime numbers indefinitely.
struct PrimeIter {
    found: Vec<u64>, // store primes that we've found so far
    candidate: u64,  // current candidate we will test for prime
}

impl PrimeIter {
    /// Creates a new PrimeIter.
    fn new() -> Self {
        // Initialize with candidate=1 so that the first call to next() yields 2.
        PrimeIter {
            found: Vec::new(),
            candidate: 1,
        }
    }

    /// Check if n is prime using division by already found primes.
    fn is_prime(&self, n: u64) -> bool {
        // Only need to check primes up to sqrt(n)
        let limit = (n as f64).sqrt();
        for &p in self.found.iter() {
            if (p as f64) > limit {
                break;
            }
            if n % p == 0 {
                return false;
            }
        }
        true
    }
}

impl Iterator for PrimeIter {
    type Item = u64;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        loop {
            // Increment candidate.
            self.candidate += 1;
            let n = self.candidate;

            // n is prime if it passes the test.
            if self.is_prime(n) {
                // Save the prime for future division testing.
                self.found.push(n);
                return Some(n);
            }
            // Continue the loop until a prime is found.
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Create our prime stream.
    let primes = PrimeIter::new();

    // Print the first 10 primes.
    for prime in primes.take(10) {
        println!("{}", prime);
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. The PrimeIter struct holds a vector of primes that have been found so far (to use for efficient trial division) and a candidate field that represents the current number being tested.

2. In the is_prime method, each candidate is tested for divisibility only by the previously found primes (up to its square root). If none divide the candidate evenly, it is deemed prime.

3. The Iterator trait’s next() function increments the candidate, tests it for primality, and when a prime is found, it is stored in the found vector and returned.

4. In main(), we use the iterator with the take(10) adapter to print the first 10 primes.

This code will indefinitely generate prime numbers, but here we only consume the first 10. You can modify it as needed for your application.

Below is a plain TypeScript example that implements a simple Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) with a single hidden layer. This example includes basic helper functions for vector/matrix operations, random initialization of weights/biases, and a forward pass that “unrolls” through a sequence of inputs. You can run or modify this example as needed.

────────────────────────────────────────────
TypeScript Code:

────────────────────────────────────────────
"use strict";

// Helper functions for matrix and vector operations

// Returns a random matrix with dimensions (rows x cols).
function randomMatrix(rows: number, cols: number): number[][] {
  const matrix: number[][] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
    const row: number[] = [];
    for (let j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
      // small random weights (from -0.05 to 0.05)
      row.push(Math.random() * 0.1 - 0.05);
    }
    matrix.push(row);
  }
  return matrix;
}

// Returns a random vector of a given length.
function randomVector(length: number): number[] {
  const vec: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < length; i++) {
    vec.push(Math.random() * 0.1 - 0.05);
  }
  return vec;
}

// Multiplies a matrix by a vector. Matrix is an array of rows.
function matVecMultiply(matrix: number[][], vector: number[]): number[] {
  const result: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < vector.length; j++) {
      sum += matrix[i][j] * vector[j];
    }
    result.push(sum);
  }
  return result;
}

// Adds two vectors elementwise.
function vectorAdd(a: number[], b: number[]): number[] {
  if (a.length !== b.length) {
    throw new Error("Vectors must have the same length");
  }
  return a.map((value, i) => value + b[i]);
}

// Applies the hyperbolic tangent activation elementwise.
function applyTanh(vector: number[]): number[] {
  return vector.map(x => Math.tanh(x));
}

//────────────────────────────────────────────
// RNN Class Implementation

class RNN {
  inputSize: number;
  hiddenSize: number;
  outputSize: number;

  // Weight matrices and bias vectors:
  // Wh: weights from input to hidden (shape: hiddenSize x inputSize)
  // Uh: weights from hidden to hidden (shape: hiddenSize x hiddenSize)
  // bh: bias for hidden layer (length: hiddenSize)
  // Wy: weights from hidden to output (shape: outputSize x hiddenSize)
  // by: bias for output layer (length: outputSize)
  Wh: number[][];
  Uh: number[][];
  bh: number[];
  Wy: number[][];
  by: number[];

  constructor(inputSize: number, hiddenSize: number, outputSize: number) {
    this.inputSize = inputSize;
    this.hiddenSize = hiddenSize;
    this.outputSize = outputSize;

    this.Wh = randomMatrix(hiddenSize, inputSize);
    this.Uh = randomMatrix(hiddenSize, hiddenSize);
    this.bh = randomVector(hiddenSize);

    this.Wy = randomMatrix(outputSize, hiddenSize);
    this.by = randomVector(outputSize);
  }

  // Forward pass: Process an input sequence where each element is an input vector.
  // inputs: number[][]  (timeSteps x inputSize)
  // Returns an object containing the outputs and all hidden states.
  forward(inputs: number[][]): { outputs: number[][], hiddenStates: number[][] } {
    // Initialize hidden state (h0) as a zero vector.
    let h: number[] = new Array(this.hiddenSize).fill(0);
    const outputs: number[][] = [];
    const hiddenStates: number[][] = [];

    // Process each time step.
    for (let t = 0; t < inputs.length; t++) {
      // Calculate the new hidden state:
      // h = tanh( Wh * x_t + Uh * h + bh )
      const wx: number[] = matVecMultiply(this.Wh, inputs[t]);
      const uh: number[] = matVecMultiply(this.Uh, h);
      const hiddenRaw: number[] = vectorAdd(vectorAdd(wx, uh), this.bh);
      h = applyTanh(hiddenRaw);
      hiddenStates.push(h);

      // Calculate the output:
      // y = Wy * h + by  (For simplicity, we are doing a linear output without activation.)
      const y: number[] = vectorAdd(matVecMultiply(this.Wy, h), this.by);
      outputs.push(y);
    }

    return { outputs, hiddenStates };
  }
}

//────────────────────────────────────────────
// Example usage:

// Define input, hidden, and output dimensions.
const inputSize = 3;
const hiddenSize = 5;
const outputSize = 2;

// Create an instance of the RNN.
const rnn = new RNN(inputSize, hiddenSize, outputSize);

// Define an example input sequence: an array of time steps, each a vector of length inputSize.
const inputSequence: number[][] = [
  [1, 0, 0],
  [0, 1, 0],
  [0, 0, 1]
];

// Pass the input sequence through the RNN.
const result = rnn.forward(inputSequence);

console.log("Outputs:");
console.log(result.outputs);

console.log("Hidden States:");
console.log(result.hiddenStates);

────────────────────────────────────────────
Explanation:

1. The helper functions (randomMatrix, randomVector, matVecMultiply, vectorAdd, applyTanh) support the basic linear algebra operations.
2. The RNN class contains randomly initialized weight matrices and biases.
3. The forward() method iterates over each input time step:
   • It calculates the hidden state h using the current input and the previous hidden state.
   • It then computes the output y for that time step.
4. Finally, the code includes a simple example that creates an RNN, feeds in a short sequence, and logs the outputs and hidden states.

This is a minimal implementation intended for educational or prototyping purposes. For more sophisticated tasks (such as training via backpropagation through time), you would need to implement gradients, loss functions, and an optimization routine.

Below is an example of a simple object‐oriented sprite editor implemented in JavaScript. In this example, the editor renders a grid (a 16×16 “sprite”) on an HTML5 canvas. You can click on any cell to fill it with the currently selected color (picked via an input). The code is organized as a SpriteEditor class that encapsulates all the logic.

You can copy the following code into a file—for example, “sprite-editor.html”—and open it in your browser:

----------------------------------------------------------
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Simple Sprite Editor</title>
    <style>
      body {
        font-family: sans-serif;
        margin: 20px;
      }
      canvas {
        border: 1px solid #333;
        image-rendering: pixelated;
      }
      #container {
        display: flex;
        gap: 20px;
      }
      #controls {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 10px;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Sprite Editor</h1>
    <div id="container">
      <canvas id="spriteCanvas" width="320" height="320"></canvas>
      <div id="controls">
        <label>
          Choose Color:
          <input type="color" id="colorPicker" value="#ff0000">
        </label>
        <button id="clearButton">Clear Sprite</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <script>
      // SpriteEditor class definition
      class SpriteEditor {
        constructor(canvas, options = {}) {
          this.canvas = canvas;
          this.ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
          this.cols = options.cols || 16;      // Number of columns in our grid
          this.rows = options.rows || 16;      // Number of rows in our grid
          this.cellSize = canvas.width / this.cols;
          // Create a 2D grid for our pixel colors (initially empty)
          this.grid = [];
          for (let y = 0; y < this.rows; y++) {
            let row = [];
            for (let x = 0; x < this.cols; x++) {
              row.push(null);
            }
            this.grid.push(row);
          }
          this.currentColor = options.currentColor || "#ff0000";
          // Bind event handlers so that "this" is maintained
          this.canvas.addEventListener('click', this.handleClick.bind(this));
          this.drawGrid();
        }

        // Draw the complete sprite (all cells and grid lines)
        drawGrid() {
          // Clear the entire canvas
          this.ctx.clearRect(0, 0, this.canvas.width, this.canvas.height);
          for (let y = 0; y < this.rows; y++) {
            for (let x = 0; x < this.cols; x++) {
              let color = this.grid[y][x];
              // If a cell has a defined color, fill it
              if (color) {
                this.ctx.fillStyle = color;
                this.ctx.fillRect(x * this.cellSize, y * this.cellSize, this.cellSize, this.cellSize);
              } else {
                // Optionally, fill with a background color, here using white
                this.ctx.fillStyle = "#fff";
                this.ctx.fillRect(x * this.cellSize, y * this.cellSize, this.cellSize, this.cellSize);
              }
              // Draw the grid cell border
              this.ctx.strokeStyle = "#ccc";
              this.ctx.strokeRect(x * this.cellSize, y * this.cellSize, this.cellSize, this.cellSize);
            }
          }
        }

        // Convert a mouse event to grid coordinates 
        getCellFromEvent(evt) {
          const rect = this.canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
          const x = evt.clientX - rect.left;
          const y = evt.clientY - rect.top;
          const col = Math.floor(x / this.cellSize);
          const row = Math.floor(y / this.cellSize);
          return { col, row };
        }

        // Toggle or set the cell's color at the clicked location
        handleClick(evt) {
          const { col, row } = this.getCellFromEvent(evt);
          // Safety-check if click is outside bounds (rare)
          if (col < 0 || col >= this.cols || row < 0 || row >= this.rows) {
            return;
          }
          // Set the cell to the current color
          this.grid[row][col] = this.currentColor;
          this.drawGrid();
        }

        // Change the current drawing color
        setColor(newColor) {
          this.currentColor = newColor;
        }

        // Clears the sprite (resets all cell values)
        clearSprite() {
          for (let y = 0; y < this.rows; y++) {
            for (let x = 0; x < this.cols; x++) {
              this.grid[y][x] = null;
            }
          }
          this.drawGrid();
        }
      }

      // Once the page is loaded, initialize our SpriteEditor instance
      window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        const canvas = document.getElementById('spriteCanvas');
        const colorPicker = document.getElementById('colorPicker');
        const clearButton = document.getElementById('clearButton');

        // Create the editor instance
        const editor = new SpriteEditor(canvas, {
          cols: 16,
          rows: 16,
          currentColor: colorPicker.value
        });

        // Update the current color when the user picks a color
        colorPicker.addEventListener('input', (evt) => {
          editor.setColor(evt.target.value);
        });

        // Wire up the clear button to reset the sprite
        clearButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
          editor.clearSprite();
        });
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
----------------------------------------------------------

Explanation of the code:

1. The HTML sets up a canvas element (320×320 pixels) which displays a 16×16 grid where each cell is 20×20 pixels. A color input and a clear button are provided in a controls panel.

2. The JavaScript defines a SpriteEditor class that:
   • Initializes a grid (2D array) representing the sprite.
   • Listens for click events on the canvas; when the canvas is clicked, it computes which cell was clicked and fills that cell with the current color.
   • Contains a drawGrid() method that redraws the grid and the colors of each cell.
   • Provides methods to change the drawing color and to clear the sprite.

3. When the document is loaded, an instance of SpriteEditor is created, and event listeners are added to the color input and clear button.

Feel free to extend this sample by adding features such as saving the sprite as an image, undo functionality, or support for multiple frames for animations. Enjoy coding your sprite editor!

Below is one way to solve the problem. In our solution the Action class is completely “data‐driven” by a configuration (which you might load from a JSON or YAML file). In our example the configuration for an action is a dictionary that specifies, for each “target” (or “actor”) of the action, what should happen. For example, a “deal damage” action might be defined by

  • reducing the “target” hit points by a fixed amount,
  • a “heal actor” action would increase the “actor” hit points,
  • and more complex actions (like life‐leech) can combine several operations and even use intermediate values (such as “last_damage” for a percentage heal).

One solution is to design the DSL as a dictionary specifying for each entity (“target” or “actor”) a small “operation” whose keys are:

  • action: the type (“damage” or “heal”),
  • amount: a number (or factor) to be applied,
  • percent (optionally): if True, then the amount is taken as a factor of some entity’s current hp,
  • source (optionally): if percent is True, then by default the “target” (or the one being modified) is used, but you may also specify “actor” or vice‐versa;
  • or source can be “last_damage” to refer to the damage actually applied to a target in a previous step (useful for life‐leech actions).

In the code below we define an Action class that accepts a config (which could be loaded from file) and then “executes” the action on a given actor and target. (Note that the Character class from your sample is unmodified.)

––––– Begin Code –––––

class Character:
    def __init__(self, char_data):
        self.name = char_data["name"]
        self.hp = char_data["hp"]
        self.damage = char_data["damage"]

# Here’s our data‐driven Action
class Action:
    def __init__(self, config):
        """
        config is a dict that may look like this (example for life leech):

        {
           "target": {"action": "damage", "amount": "10"},
           "actor":  {"action": "heal",   "amount": "0.5", "source": "last_damage"}
        }

        Other examples:
          • Deal damage:
               { "target": { "action": "damage", "amount": "15" } }
          • Heal actor:
               { "actor": { "action": "heal", "amount": "10" } }
          • Heal target:
               { "target": { "action": "heal", "amount": "10" } }
          • Deal damage based on portion of target’s hp:
               { "target": { "action": "damage", "amount": "0.2", "percent": True } }
          • Deal damage based on portion of actor’s hp with backlash on actor:
               { "target": { "action": "damage", "amount": "0.3", "percent": True, "source": "actor" },
                 "actor":  { "action": "damage", "amount": "0.1", "percent": True, "source": "actor" } }
        """
        self.config = config

    def execute(self, actor, target):
        # Create a context that holds the characters and any temporary values.
        context = {"actor": actor, "target": target, "last_damage": None}
        # For our DSL we assume that any target operation must be executed first.
        # This matters if later operations (e.g. on actor) refer to the damage applied.
        if "target" in self.config:
            damage_result = self.apply_operation(entity=target, details=self.config["target"], context=context)
            # if target took damage, record the value (this is used by actions like life leech)
            if self.config["target"].get("action") == "damage":
                context["last_damage"] = damage_result

        if "actor" in self.config:
            # Execute the actor’s operation (it might be heal or even damage, as in #5 in your example)
            self.apply_operation(entity=actor, details=self.config["actor"], context=context)

    def apply_operation(self, entity, details, context):
        """
        Given an entity (actor or target) and an operation specification (details),
        compute the amount (using percent factors if requested) and perform the action.
        Returns the computed amount so that it can be referenced by other operations.
        """
        # Get the base numerical value. We assume that amount is given as a string.
        base_val = float(details["amount"])

        # By default, if percent is requested, the value is computed on the entity itself.
        if details.get("percent", False):
            # If there is a specific "source", use that (e.g., actor’s hp vs target’s hp)
            if "source" in details:
                if details["source"] == "actor":
                    source_entity = context["actor"]
                elif details["source"] == "target":
                    source_entity = context["target"]
                else:
                    # default back if unknown source
                    source_entity = entity
            else:
                source_entity = entity
            computed_amount = source_entity.hp * base_val
        # If the source is "last_damage" then we use that value multiplied by given factor.
        elif details.get("source", None) == "last_damage":
            computed_amount = context.get("last_damage", 0) * base_val
        else:
            # Use the static numerical value.
            computed_amount = base_val

        action_type = details["action"]
        if action_type == "damage":
            entity.hp -= computed_amount
            print(f"{entity.name} takes {computed_amount} damage, new hp: {entity.hp}")
        elif action_type == "heal":
            entity.hp += computed_amount
            print(f"{entity.name} heals {computed_amount} hp, new hp: {entity.hp}")
        else:
            print(f"Unknown action type: {action_type}")
        return computed_amount


# --- Example usage ---

# Create two characters.
actor = Character({"name": "Hero", "hp": 100, "damage": 20})
target = Character({"name": "Goblin", "hp": 50, "damage": 5})

# Define several action configurations:

# 1. Deal a fixed 15 damage to target.
deal_damage_config = {
    "target": {"action": "damage", "amount": "15"}
}

# 2. Heal actor by 10.
heal_actor_config = {
    "actor": {"action": "heal", "amount": "10"}
}

# 3. Heal target by 10.
heal_target_config = {
    "target": {"action": "heal", "amount": "10"}
}

# 4. Deal damage to target based on a portion (20%) of target's current hp.
damage_percent_target_config = {
    "target": {"action": "damage", "amount": "0.2", "percent": True}
}

# 5. Deal damage to target based on 30% of actor's current hp and also damage actor for 10% of actor's hp.
damage_percent_actor_config = {
    "target": {"action": "damage", "amount": "0.3", "percent": True, "source": "actor"},
    "actor":  {"action": "damage", "amount": "0.1", "percent": True, "source": "actor"}
}

# 6. Life leech: Deal fixed 10 damage to target and heal actor for 50% of that damage.
life_leech_config = {
    "target": {"action": "damage", "amount": "10"},
    "actor":  {"action": "heal", "amount": "0.5", "source": "last_damage"}
}

# Run one of the actions as an example.
print("== Action: Life Leech ==")
action = Action(life_leech_config)
action.execute(actor, target)

# You can test the other actions in the same manner.
# For example:
print("\n== Action: Deal Damage ==")
action2 = Action(deal_damage_config)
action2.execute(actor, target)

print("\n== Action: Heal Actor ==")
action3 = Action(heal_actor_config)
action3.execute(actor, target)

# In a real-world system the configuration would be loaded from external files
# and the DSL could be further extended to support more kinds of operations.

# End of implementation.

––––– End Code –––––

Explanation:

1. The Action class is initialized with a config dictionary that describes what should be done. It contains keys like "target" and/or "actor" that specify operations.

2. The execute(actor, target) method builds a context that can later be used to refer to computation results (like damage from target, if needed) and then applies the configured operations.

3. The apply_operation method computes the change amount. If the operation is percent-based, it uses a “source” field to decide which hp to multiply; it also checks for a special “last_damage” source. Then it applies the operation by subtracting (for damage) or adding (for healing) from the entity’s hp.

4. Several example configurations are provided. You can add, remove, or extend these in an external config file or dictionary as needed.

This implementation demonstrates one way to design a general, data-driven Action class powered by a simple DSL. You may extend this solution (for example, to support additional character properties or more complex arithmetic expressions) as your game grows.

Below is an example of how you can combine @tanstack/vue-query with a global Pinia store to call an endpoint (/get_session), extract only the keys you care about, and update your store. In this example, we’ll:

1. Define a global Pinia session store.
2. Create a query function to fetch your session data.
3. Use useQuery with the select option to pick only the desired keys.
4. Use the onSuccess callback (or the select option) to update your Pinia store.

──────────────────────────────
1. Create a Pinia Store
──────────────────────────────
For example, you might have a Pinia store (e.g., sessionStore.js) that looks like this:

------------------------------------------------------------
import { defineStore } from 'pinia'

export const useSessionStore = defineStore('session', {
  state: () => ({
    // define the keys that you expect from the session response
    userId: null,
    userRole: null,
    // you can add more keys as needed
  }),
  actions: {
    setSessionData(data) {
      // Update only the keys you need
      this.userId = data.userId
      this.userRole = data.userRole
    }
  }
})
------------------------------------------------------------

──────────────────────────────
2. Create a Query Function and Use Vue-Query
──────────────────────────────
In your component (or a composable), import useQuery from @tanstack/vue-query, axios (or your preferred http client), and your session store. Then set up the query as follows:

------------------------------------------------------------
<script setup>
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/vue-query'
import axios from 'axios'
import { useSessionStore } from '@/stores/sessionStore'

// Define a function to fetch the session info from your API
const fetchSession = async () => {
  const response = await axios.get('/get_session')
  return response.data
}

// Access your global session store
const sessionStore = useSessionStore()

// Use the query hook
const { data, error, isLoading } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['session'],        // unique key for caching this query
  queryFn: fetchSession,         // function to fetch data
  // Use "select" to pick only the keys you want.
  select: (data) => ({
    userId: data.userId,
    userRole: data.userRole
    // you can add as many properties as necessary
  }),
  // onSuccess is a good place for side effects like updating your Pinia store.
  onSuccess: (selectedData) => {
    sessionStore.setSessionData(selectedData)
  }
})
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <div v-if="isLoading">Loading session...</div>
    <div v-else-if="error">Error loading session</div>
    <div v-else>
      Logged in user: {{ sessionStore.userId }} with role: {{ sessionStore.userRole }}
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
------------------------------------------------------------

──────────────────────────────
How It Works
──────────────────────────────
• The fetchSession function makes a GET request to /get_session and returns the relevant data.
• The useQuery call uses a unique key (['session']) and calls fetchSession to get the session data.
• The select option extracts only the userId and userRole keys from the response.
• The onSuccess callback then calls sessionStore.setSessionData with the selected keys; as a result, your global store updates and can be accessed throughout your application.
• Finally, your template is reactive to changes in the Pinia store.

──────────────────────────────
Notes
──────────────────────────────
• While you can perform side effects inside select, it’s typically best to use onSuccess if you’re updating external state (like a global store) so as to keep the select function pure.
• If you prefer to use a watcher on the returned data instead of onSuccess, that’s also an option. For example, you could watch the data variable and update the store when it changes.
• Ensure that Pinia and Vue Query are properly installed and set up according to your project’s requirements.

This setup provides a clean way to use Vue Query’s data fetching and transformation features to keep your global Pinia store in sync with your session data.

