Below is an example of a simple iMessage app extension that, when active, displays two buttons at the bottom of its view (which appears above the keyboard in an iMessage conversation). This example uses Swift and a basic view‐setup in the extension’s main view controller (a subclass of MSMessagesAppViewController).

Note that you must have Xcode installed and create a new “iMessage Extension” project (or add an iMessage target to an existing project) before adding the code below. In a new iMessage Extension project, replace the contents of MessagesViewController.swift with the code provided.

─────────────────────────────
// MessagesViewController.swift
─────────────────────────────

import UIKit
import Messages

class MessagesViewController: MSMessagesAppViewController {

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        view.backgroundColor = .white

        // Create the two buttons
        let button1 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button1.setTitle("Button 1", for: .normal)
        button1.titleLabel?.font = UIFont.systemFont(ofSize: 17, weight: .medium)
        button1.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button1Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        let button2 = UIButton(type: .system)
        button2.setTitle("Button 2", for: .normal)
        button2.titleLabel?.font = UIFont.systemFont(ofSize: 17, weight: .medium)
        button2.addTarget(self, action: #selector(button2Tapped), for: .touchUpInside)

        // Arrange the buttons in a horizontal stack view
        let stackView = UIStackView(arrangedSubviews: [button1, button2])
        stackView.axis = .horizontal
        stackView.spacing = 20
        stackView.alignment = .center
        stackView.distribution = .fillEqually
        stackView.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false

        view.addSubview(stackView)

        // Add constraints to position the stack view above the keyboard area.
        // Here we place it centered horizontally and near the bottom safe area.
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            stackView.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerXAnchor),
            stackView.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.safeAreaLayoutGuide.bottomAnchor, constant: -10),
            stackView.heightAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 44),
            stackView.leadingAnchor.constraint(greaterThanOrEqualTo: view.leadingAnchor, constant: 20),
            stackView.trailingAnchor.constraint(lessThanOrEqualTo: view.trailingAnchor, constant: -20)
        ])
    }

    // Button action handlers
    @objc func button1Tapped() {
        // Replace with your own functionality.
        print("Button 1 tapped")

        // For example, you might want to insert a message or change presentation style.
        // Here we simply print to the console.
    }

    @objc func button2Tapped() {
        // Replace with your own functionality.
        print("Button 2 tapped")

        // Here you might transition between compact and expanded presentation styles:
        // if self.activeConversation != nil {
        //     if self.presentationStyle == .expanded {
        //         self.requestPresentationStyle(.compact)
        //     } else {
        //         self.requestPresentationStyle(.expanded)
        //     }
        // }
    }

    // MARK: - Conversation Handling (Optional)

    override func didBecomeActive(with conversation: MSConversation) {
        // Called when the extension becomes active — use this method to trigger UI updates.
    }

    override func willResignActive(with conversation: MSConversation) {
        // Called when the extension is about to move from active to inactive.
    }

    // Additional lifecycle methods (willTransition, didTransition) can be implemented if needed.
}

─────────────────────────────

Explanation:

1. The project’s default view controller is subclassed from MSMessagesAppViewController. In viewDidLoad(), we set up a white background and add two UIButton instances.

2. We create a horizontal UIStackView to layout the buttons evenly. The stack view is then constrained to be centered horizontally and anchored near the bottom safe area of the view (so it appears above the keyboard/input field).

3. Each button is given an action method (@objc) so that tapping them prints a simple message in the console. In a production app, you’d replace these print statements with functionality such as inserting text, stickers, or switching between presentation styles.

4. Finally, there are stubs for the iMessage lifecycle methods (didBecomeActive(with:), etc.) that you can use to update your UI depending on the active conversation.

To run the code:
• Create or open your iMessage extension project in Xcode.
• Replace the existing code in MessagesViewController.swift with the code above.
• Select the iMessage extension scheme and run it in the simulator or on a device.
• When the extension appears in an iMessage conversation, you’ll see the two buttons above the system keyboard.

This simple example should give you a starting point to further customize your iMessage extension’s UI and functionality.

You can use JavaScript in your browser console to fetch a page’s content and trigger a download. For example, you can use the fetch API to get the page as a Blob and then create a temporary link with a download attribute. Note that this works if the target URL permits cross-origin requests (or if it’s on the same domain).

Here’s an example snippet you can run in your browser’s console:

------------------------------------------------------------
(function directDownload(url, filename = 'download.html') {
  fetch(url)
    .then(response => {
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
      }
      return response.blob();
    })
    .then(blob => {
      const blobUrl = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      const a = document.createElement('a');
      a.style.display = 'none';
      a.href = blobUrl;
      a.download = filename; // specify the filename for download
      document.body.appendChild(a);
      a.click();
      // Clean up and remove the object URL
      window.URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl);
      a.remove();
    })
    .catch(error => console.error('Error during download:', error));
})('https://example.com/page-to-download.html');
------------------------------------------------------------

How it works:
1. The script uses fetch to get the content from the specified URL.
2. It converts the response to a Blob.
3. A temporary anchor element (a) is created and configured with href set to the blob URL and a download attribute specifying the filename.
4. The link is added to the document and programmatically clicked to trigger the download.
5. Finally, the blob URL is revoked and the temporary element is removed.

Adjust the URL and filename as needed. Remember that if the URL is cross-domain and doesn’t allow CORS, the fetch request might fail due to security restrictions.

This method should work directly in your browser console.

Below is an example Rust program that reads a list of URLs from a file (specified as a command‐line argument), separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and then downloads each YouTube URL by calling yt-dlp via a subprocess. You can compile and run this with Cargo.

Create a file (for example, main.rs) with the following contents:

------------------------------------------------------------
use std::env;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;

// Helper function to determine if a URL is a YouTube URL.
fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    // A basic check: you can extend this as needed.
    url.contains("youtube.com") || url.contains("youtu.be")
}

fn main() {
    // Ensure we got the file path as a command-line argument.
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 {
        eprintln!("Usage: {} <url_file>", args[0]);
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
    let file_path = &args[1];

    // Open the file for reading.
    let file = match File::open(file_path) {
        Ok(f) => f,
        Err(e) => {
            eprintln!("Error opening {}: {}", file_path, e);
            std::process::exit(1);
        }
    };
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    // Vectors to store separated URLs.
    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::new();

    // Read each line from the file.
    for line in reader.lines() {
        match line {
            Ok(url) => {
                let url = url.trim();
                if url.is_empty() {
                    continue;
                }
                if is_youtube_url(url) {
                    youtube_urls.push(url.to_string());
                } else {
                    other_urls.push(url.to_string());
                }
            }
            Err(e) => eprintln!("Error reading line: {}", e),
        }
    }

    println!("Found {} YouTube URLs", youtube_urls.len());
    println!("Found {} other URLs", other_urls.len());

    // For each YouTube URL, call yt-dlp.
    for url in youtube_urls {
        println!("Downloading: {}", url);
        let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(&url)
            .status(); // Using status() to just check exit code

        match status {
            Ok(s) if s.success() => {
                println!("Successfully downloaded: {}", url);
            }
            Ok(s) => {
                eprintln!(
                    "yt-dlp returned a non-zero exit status ({}).",
                    s.code().unwrap_or(-1)
                );
            }
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Failed to execute yt-dlp for {}: {}", url, e);
            }
        }
    }
}
------------------------------------------------------------

Usage Instructions:

1. Make sure you have Rust installed. If not, install it from https://rust-lang.org.

2. Create a new Cargo project (if you haven’t already):
   • cargo new youtube_downloader
   • Replace the contents of src/main.rs with the code above.

3. Create a text file (for example, urls.txt) that contains URLs (one per line), such as:
   https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
   https://example.com

4. Ensure yt-dlp is installed and available on your PATH (see https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp).

5. Build and run the project:
   • cargo run -- urls.txt

The program will report how many YouTube URLs were found and will download each one using yt-dlp. Other URLs are simply listed (in this example, we do not process them further, but you could extend the code to handle them as needed).

This example should help you get started with file I/O, string processing, and invoking external commands in Rust.

Below is an example C# console application that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached. Be sure to update the certificate path, password, URL, and request content as needed for your scenario.

-------------------------------------------------
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace PostRequestWithClientCert
{
    class Program
    {
        // Note: This demo uses C# 7.1 or later (async Main)
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Path to your client certificate (PFX file) and its password
            string certificatePath = "path/to/your/certificate.pfx";
            string certificatePassword = "your_certificate_password";

            // Load the certificate from file
            X509Certificate2 clientCertificate;
            try
            {
                clientCertificate = new X509Certificate2(certificatePath, certificatePassword);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Error loading certificate: " + ex.Message);
                return;
            }

            // Create an HttpClientHandler and attach the certificate
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCertificate);

            // Optional: if the server certificate is self-signed, you may need to override the certificate validation
            // handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (message, cert, chain, errors) => true;

            // Create HttpClient using the handler
            using (var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler))
            {
                // Specify the target URL
                var url = "https://yourserver.com/api/resource";

                // Create the content to be sent in the POST request.
                // For example, here we're sending a JSON payload.
                var jsonData = "{\"key\":\"value\"}";
                var content = new StringContent(jsonData, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

                try
                {
                    // Send the POST request
                    HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.PostAsync(url, content);

                    // Ensure the response indicates success
                    if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
                    {
                        string responseContent = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                        Console.WriteLine("Response from server: " + responseContent);
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        Console.WriteLine($"Request failed. Status code: {response.StatusCode}");
                        string errorContent = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                        Console.WriteLine("Error details: " + errorContent);
                    }
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("An error occurred while sending the request: " + ex.Message);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
-------------------------------------------------

How It Works:

1. The client certificate is loaded from a PFX file using X509Certificate2.
2. An HttpClientHandler is created and the certificate is added to its ClientCertificates collection.
3. An HttpClient is instantiated with the custom handler.
4. A POST request is sent to the provided URL with JSON content.
5. The response is read and displayed.

Make sure your certificate file is accessible and that the URL and any necessary headers or content types match your server’s requirements.

Below is an example of how you can extract hashtags from a text string in C# using regular expressions. This example uses the Regex class with the pattern "#\w+" to match any word that starts with the '#' character:

--------------------------------------------------
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string text = "This is a #sample text with #multiple #hashtags, including #CSharp and #Regex.";

        List<string> hashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);

        foreach (string hashtag in hashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
        }
    }

    static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        List<string> result = new List<string>();

        // Regex pattern: '#' followed by one or more word characters (letters, digits, or underscores)
        Regex regex = new Regex(@"#\w+");

        MatchCollection matches = regex.Matches(text);
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            result.Add(match.Value);
        }
        return result;
    }
}

--------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. The regular expression @"#\w+" matches:
   - '#' the literal hashtag symbol.
   - "\w+" one or more alphanumeric characters (and underscore). Modify this if you need to support additional characters.
2. The Matches method returns all matches found in the input text.
3. We then iterate through these matches and add each hashtag to the list.

This code will print:
   #sample
   #multiple
   #hashtags
   #CSharp
   #Regex

You can adjust the regex pattern according to your hashtag rules if needed (e.g., allowing dashes or other symbols).

