One of the easiest ways is to use Nix’s package set for LLVM. For example, you can simply run:

  nix-shell -p llvmPackages.llvm_17

This command downloads a shell environment with LLVM version 17 (and its associated tools) available.

If you’d prefer to have a reproducible development environment (for example, by using a shell.nix file), you can create a file named shell.nix with contents like:

  { pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:
  pkgs.mkShell {
    buildInputs = [
      pkgs.llvmPackages.llvm_17
    ];
  }

Then, running nix-shell in the directory will drop you into an environment where LLVM 17 is available.

Note that depending on your Nixpkgs revision, the package might be named slightly differently (for example, older versions sometimes reference llvmPackages_17). If you run into issues, you might need to adjust the attribute accordingly.

Both approaches ensure you have a shell with LLVM 17 installed without polluting your global environment.

Below is an overview and sample code that shows one way to write a Windows service that uses npcap to capture (and “decode”) network traffic. (Decoding here means that after capturing raw packets you inspect their headers and payloads.) This example is in C/C++ and assumes you have installed npcap (and its SDK or development libraries) and are familiar with basic Windows service programming. The following steps and code samples are provided as a starting point—you’ll need to adapt and extend them for your needs.

─────────────────────────────  
Overview of the Steps

1. • Install npcap and set up the development environment.  
  – Ensure npcap is installed (https://nmap.org/npcap/)  
  – Link against the npcap lib (usually the same API as WinPcap) so you can include “pcap.h.”

2. • Create a Windows service in C/C++ by using the Windows Service API.  
  – Write a service “main” (ServiceMain) that registers a control handler (ServiceCtrlHandlerEx).  
  – Implement service start-up, stop, and logging routines.

3. • Inside your service’s main thread or a worker thread, open a network device for live capture using npcap functions.  
  – Use pcap_findalldevs() to enumerate available devices  
  – Open a chosen device with pcap_open_live()  
  – Start an infinite loop (or a worker thread loop) to capture packets with pcap_loop() or pcap_dispatch()

4. • In the packet callback, decode the packet contents as needed.  
  – Parse the packet header (pcap_pkthdr) and raw data  
  – For example, you might decode Ethernet, IP, TCP/UDP headers  
  – Log or process the information

5. • Handle proper shutdown––stop the capture loop gracefully and clean up resources when the Windows service stops.

─────────────────────────────  
Example Code

Below is a simple example that demonstrates the skeleton of such a service. Note that error checking is minimal and you’ll likely need to add robust logging, graceful exit routines, and additional “decoding” logic.

-------------------------
// Include Windows, npcap headers and standard libraries
#include <windows.h>
#include <winsvc.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include "pcap.h"

// Global service status variables
SERVICE_STATUS          g_ServiceStatus = {0};
SERVICE_STATUS_HANDLE   g_StatusHandle = NULL;
HANDLE                  g_ServiceStopEvent = INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE;

// Forward declarations
void WINAPI ServiceMain(DWORD argc, LPTSTR* argv);
void WINAPI ServiceCtrlHandler(DWORD CtrlCode);
DWORD WINAPI CaptureThread(LPVOID lpParam);
void PacketHandler(u_char* param, const struct pcap_pkthdr* header, const u_char* pkt_data);

// Entry point for the executable. When installed as a service the SCM will call ServiceMain.
int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    SERVICE_TABLE_ENTRY ServiceTable[] = 
    {
        { "MyNpcapService", ServiceMain },
        { NULL, NULL }
    };

    if (!StartServiceCtrlDispatcher(ServiceTable))
    {
        // Running from console; for debugging you could call ServiceMain directly.
        printf("StartServiceCtrlDispatcher error = %d\n", GetLastError());
        return GetLastError();
    }

    return 0;
}

// Service entry point
void WINAPI ServiceMain(DWORD argc, LPTSTR* argv)
{
    DWORD Status = E_FAIL;

    // Register our service control handler function
    g_StatusHandle = RegisterServiceCtrlHandler("MyNpcapService", ServiceCtrlHandler);
    if (g_StatusHandle == NULL) 
    {
        return;
    }

    // Initialize service status structure
    ZeroMemory(&g_ServiceStatus, sizeof(g_ServiceStatus));
    g_ServiceStatus.dwServiceType = SERVICE_WIN32_OWN_PROCESS;
    g_ServiceStatus.dwControlsAccepted = SERVICE_ACCEPT_STOP;
    g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_START_PENDING;
    g_ServiceStatus.dwWin32ExitCode = 0;
    g_ServiceStatus.dwServiceSpecificExitCode = 0;
    g_ServiceStatus.dwCheckPoint = 0;

    // Create an event to signal the service to stop.
    g_ServiceStopEvent = CreateEvent(NULL, TRUE, FALSE, NULL);
    if (g_ServiceStopEvent == NULL)
    {
        g_ServiceStatus.dwControlsAccepted = 0;
        g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_STOPPED;
        g_ServiceStatus.dwWin32ExitCode = GetLastError();
        SetServiceStatus(g_StatusHandle, &g_ServiceStatus);
        return;
    }

    // Report running status when initialization is complete.
    g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_RUNNING;
    SetServiceStatus(g_StatusHandle, &g_ServiceStatus);

    // Start the network capture thread.
    HANDLE hThread = CreateThread(NULL, 0, CaptureThread, NULL, 0, NULL);

    // Main service loop: wait for stop signal.
    WaitForSingleObject(g_ServiceStopEvent, INFINITE);

    // Cleanup: signal the capture thread to exit, join thread, and clean up handles
    // (You might need to signal the pcap_loop to break or call pcap_breakloop inside CaptureThread.)
    if (hThread)
    {
        // Wait for thread to finish or call TerminateThread (not recommended)
        WaitForSingleObject(hThread, INFINITE);
        CloseHandle(hThread);
    }

    CloseHandle(g_ServiceStopEvent);

    // Tell the service controller that the service is stopped.
    g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_STOPPED;
    SetServiceStatus(g_StatusHandle, &g_ServiceStatus);
}

// Service control handler: handles stop commands, etc.
void WINAPI ServiceCtrlHandler(DWORD CtrlCode)
{
    switch(CtrlCode)
    {
        case SERVICE_CONTROL_STOP:
            if (g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState != SERVICE_RUNNING)
                break;

            // Signal the service to stop.
            g_ServiceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_STOP_PENDING;
            SetServiceStatus(g_StatusHandle, &g_ServiceStatus);

            SetEvent(g_ServiceStopEvent);
            break;

        default:
            break;
    }
}

// This thread runs the network capture.
// It initializes npcap capture, enters a loop that processes packets until signalled to stop.
DWORD WINAPI CaptureThread(LPVOID lpParam)
{
    char errbuf[PCAP_ERRBUF_SIZE];
    pcap_if_t *alldevs;

    // Find all network devices
    if (pcap_findalldevs(&alldevs, errbuf) == -1)
    {
        // Log error (you might log to Event Viewer or a file)
        return 1;
    }

    // For demonstration, pick the first device. In production, allow configuration.
    pcap_if_t *device = alldevs;
    if (device == NULL)
    {
        // No devices available.
        pcap_freealldevs(alldevs);
        return 1;
    }

    // Open the device - 65536 byte capture, promiscuous mode, 1 sec timeout.
    pcap_t* fp = pcap_open_live(device->name, 65536, 1, 1000, errbuf);
    if (fp == NULL)
    {
        // Log error opening device.
        pcap_freealldevs(alldevs);
        return 1;
    }

    // Free the device list as we no longer need it.
    pcap_freealldevs(alldevs);

    // Capture loop:
    // You can run pcap_loop in a separate thread; to stop it gracefully,
    // you can call pcap_breakloop() when g_ServiceStopEvent is signaled.
    while (WaitForSingleObject(g_ServiceStopEvent, 0) != WAIT_OBJECT_0)
    {
        // Dispatch one packet (timeout-based or use pcap_next_ex in a loop)
        int ret = pcap_dispatch(fp, 1, PacketHandler, NULL);
        if (ret == -1)
        {
            // Error handling; possibly break the loop
            break;
        }
        // You can add Sleep() for throttle or continue immediately.
    }

    // If using pcap_loop, you would call pcap_breakloop(fp) to break out of the loop.
    pcap_close(fp);
    return 0;
}

// PacketHandler: Called for every captured packet.
// Here you can decode the raw packet data.
void PacketHandler(u_char* param, const struct pcap_pkthdr* header, const u_char* pkt_data)
{
    // Example: print packet capture length info to a log (or Event Viewer)
    // You might parse Ethernet, IP and TCP/UDP headers here.
    char timebuf[64];
    SYSTEMTIME st;
    GetLocalTime(&st);
    sprintf(timebuf, "%02d:%02d:%02d.%03d", st.wHour, st.wMinute, st.wSecond, st.wMilliseconds);

    // Minimal logging: You’d need to implement a thread-safe logging mechanism.
    FILE* logFile = fopen("C:\\MyNpcapService.log", "a+");
    if (logFile)
    {
        fprintf(logFile, "[%s] Packet captured: length %d bytes\n", timebuf, header->len);
        fclose(logFile);
    }

    // Here you could add code to inspect pkt_data; for example:
    // • Extract Ethernet header (first 14 bytes on Ethernet)
    // • Decode IP header and then TCP/UDP header depending on protocol
    // • Log relevant fields.
}

-------------------------

─────────────────────────────  
Notes and Next Steps

▪ Privileges: Capturing packets generally requires Administrator privileges. When running as a service, ensure that the account has sufficient permission (or use npcap’s “Admin-only mode” settings).

▪ Service Installation: To install the service, you can write an installer that calls CreateService() via the Service Control Manager (SCM), or use sc.exe in an elevated command prompt:
  sc create MyNpcapService binPath= "C:\Path\To\YourExecutable.exe"

▪ Decoding Logic: The PacketHandler() function shown above only logs packet lengths. To truly “decode” network traffic, you’d add code to parse the protocols you’re interested in. You might choose to use existing libraries or write your own parsers.

▪ Robustness: In a production environment add proper error logging (for example, to the Windows Event Log), more extensive error checking, and a means to restart the capture thread if necessary.

▪ Debugging: Running services can be challenging to debug. Develop and test your capture code in a debug console application before integrating it into a service.

─────────────────────────────  
Conclusion

This example shows one approach to writing a Windows service that uses npcap to capture and decode network traffic. You’ll need to extend the sample to include your particular decoding logic, robust error handling, and logging. With this foundation, you can build a service that processes network traffic as required by your application.

To get the bounding box of a deeply nested SVG element using JavaScript, you can use the built-in getBBox() function. This function returns an object with x, y coordinates along with width and height properties for the element. Here’s a simple example:

-------------------------------------------------
let nestedElement = document.querySelector('#yourNestedElementID');
let bbox = nestedElement.getBBox();
console.log('x:', bbox.x, 'y:', bbox.y, 'width:', bbox.width, 'height:', bbox.height);
-------------------------------------------------

In this example, replace "#yourNestedElementID" with the actual ID or selector for your nested element. The getBBox() function will calculate the bounding box based on the coordinate system of the SVG container.

Keep in mind that if your element has transforms applied (like scale, rotate, etc.), the getBBox() might not match what you see on the screen directly. For more complex cases, you might need to apply matrix transformations to account for these changes. However, for many simple cases, getBBox() does the job well and provides the needed measurements.

You can achieve this kind of responsive behavior in Jetpack Compose by using the BoxWithConstraints composable. BoxWithConstraints gives you access to its parent's constraints (like maxWidth and maxHeight), which lets you decide the layout based on how much space is available—much like container queries on the web.

Below is an example of how you might create a composable that switches its layout (and even its content) based on the available width:

------------------------------------------------------------
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.*
import androidx.compose.material3.*
import androidx.compose.runtime.Composable
import androidx.compose.ui.Modifier
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.dp

@Composable
fun ResponsiveLayoutExample() {
    // BoxWithConstraints provides you the maxWidth and maxHeight
    BoxWithConstraints(
        modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize()
    ) {
        // Use a threshold to decide which layout to use.
        val isCompact = maxWidth < 600.dp

        if (isCompact) {
            // For small widths, stack contents vertically
            Column(
                modifier = Modifier
                    .fillMaxWidth()
                    .padding(16.dp)
            ) {
                Text("This is a compact layout", style = MaterialTheme.typography.headlineSmall)
                Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(8.dp))
                // Place your child composables for a compact layout here.
                Button(onClick = {}) {
                    Text("Action")
                }
            }
        } else {
            // For larger widths, place the content in a row or a more complex layout.
            Row(
                modifier = Modifier
                    .fillMaxWidth()
                    .padding(16.dp),
                horizontalArrangement = Arrangement.SpaceBetween
            ) {
                Text("This is an expanded layout", style = MaterialTheme.typography.headlineSmall)
                // You can reposition your children differently based on the available space.
                Button(onClick = {}) {
                    Text("Action")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------

In this code:

• BoxWithConstraints gives you information on the maxWidth and maxHeight available to the composable.
• We define a threshold (in this case, 600.dp) to decide whether we're in a "compact" or "expanded" scenario.
• Depending on the size check, we arrange contents in either a Column or a Row and even change the text or other content as needed.

You can adjust the threshold and the layout details as per your design requirements.

This approach is very flexible and composable-driven, mirroring the idea behind container queries in web development by allowing you to adapt the UI based on the space available.
